/* Wau-Bun — Part 3, the same scenes retold in a tauter, more
   immediate voice: the events, people, places and turns of the 1856 text,
   in present-day English, with nothing added. Original prose written for
   this app — nothing is quoted from any other book.
   Generated — do not hand-edit; the passages live in wau-bun/dark/<scene>.txt.
   Loaded only when a reader opens this mode. */
var WAUBUN_DARK_PART3 = {
  p3s1: ["Most of the command had already gone ahead of us. A day or two before we were to leave, they had started overland for Green Bay by way of Fort Winnebago, driving the cattle that belonged to the officers and the soldiers. Lieutenant Foster stayed behind. He wanted the pleasure of traveling with our party and was willing to wait for it. We waited too — long enough to see the rest of our family safely aboard the Napoleon. After that there was nothing left to hold us. We turned our faces homeward again.", "We took a little bound-girl with us. Josette, ten years old, quick and bright and pretty, the daughter of Ouilmette — a Frenchman who had been living here at the time of the Massacre — and of a Pottowattamie mother. She had been schooled at the St. Joseph's mission under Mr. McCoy. The idea that she was going all the way to the Portage with Monsieur and Madame John made her shine.", "We had a negro boy as well. Harry. Mr. Kercheval had brought him up from Kentucky the year before, and in the crossing from a slave State to a free one, Harry's position quietly changed its shape: he could be nothing now but an indentured servant. He was about to enter Dr. Wolcott's household, and before that could happen he had to choose a guardian. They brought him into the parlor, where the family had gathered, and explained it to him. My husband was a young man then, home on a visit. \"Now, Harry,\" they said to him, \"you must choose your guardian.\" Every person in that room expected the boy to name whichever of them carried the most years and the most dignity. Harry rolled his great eyes around the circle of faces. He let his head hang over to one side. Then he said, \"I'll have Master John for my guardian.\"", "From that day forward Harry considered himself, in some measure, to belong to Master John — and when the family in Chicago broke up, he came over to our establishment, which surprised nobody.", "There were three ladies in the traveling party: our mother, our sister Mrs. Helm, and myself. I had spent some of the last days of the visit thinking about the sun and the prairie wind and what they do to a face left out in them, and I had made each of us a mask out of brown linen, cut to fit — openings for the eyes, the nose, the mouth, shaped to our own features. Then, because there was no reason to stop at merely strange, I had worked eyebrows and eyelashes into them in black silk, and a black ring around the hole for the mouth. They gathered in plaits under the chin and tied above and below, and they shut out the sun and the wind completely. Nothing more frightful can be imagined than the three of us standing there fully equipped. We argued about it. The argument was over which of us should be called the ugliest.", "We left in a wash of good wishes and laughter from the few acquaintances we had left. The wagon had a pair of excellent traveling horses; sister Margaret and I had the best pacers the country could produce; and we rode off toward the Aux Plaines in high spirits, with our old friend Billy Caldwell — the Sau-ga-nash — and our brother Robert and Gholson Kercheval riding out with us as far as that."],
  p3s2: ["There was almost nobody at Barney Lawton's when we got there — a Frenchman, and a small number of Indians. My sister and I got down and went into the house. The door stood open. Two Indians sat on the floor, smoking, and they lifted their eyes as we came in. I will never forget what came over both their faces. Wonder first, and then horror. The lips of one of them went slack and his pipe dropped out of them onto the floor. Their eyes seemed to be starting out of their heads. They raised their hands, spread wide, as though to push us back out of the room, and said, slowly, \"Manitou!\" A spirit.", "We pulled up our masks and came forward smiling, with our hands out, and they were on their feet in an instant, letting out a cry of pure delight at the sight of two faces they knew.", "\"Bon-jour, bon-jour, Maman!\" And they went straight out the door to tell the others what had just happened to them.", "Our ride that afternoon lay over a prairie that ran away to the northeast with nothing living on the whole broad face of it. Nothing on the ground, at least. Over our heads flew and circled uncountable flocks of curlews, screaming their wild notes to the listening waste.", "Their cry is shrill and particular — crack, crack, crack, rackety, rackety, rackety — and coming out of dozens of throats at once, sometimes stooping down quite close to our ears, it became after a while almost more than we could stand. They seemed to have lost their senses over the excitement of so unusual and splendid a procession moving through their desolate country."],
  p3s3: ["The horses picked up their pace as we came toward a wooded knoll, and that was how we knew where we would be sleeping. They always know. Some instinct in them reads the ground and tells them where a camp belongs, and this one was as lovely a spot as anything I could have imagined.", "Down near the lake the cold, late season had kept the trees a pale yellow, the color of goslings not yet fledged. Here they were breaking into full leaf. The ground under them was carpeted so thickly with flowers that we could hardly stand to think of what we were about to do to it — fell a tree, pitch a tent, put our weight down in the middle of all that. The birds were giving out their sweetest notes in the warm, lingering light. The opening buds of the young hickory and the sassafras had filled all the air around us with perfume.", "After a day's ride in the glare of the sun, nothing could have been better than this. The children lost their heads over it. They wanted to be useful and kept inventing ways to be — stacking the saddles, breaking boughs for the fire, running the little kettles down for water and carrying them back to Petaille and Lecuyer, the Frenchmen, who were getting our supper together.", "What entertained them past all bearing was how ridiculously the horses moved once they had been spancelled. Everything was new to our little nephew Edwin. Josette had already made more than one horseback trip to St. Joseph, and she took on all the dignity of a seasoned traveler, explaining to him whatever struck him as strange or unaccountable.", "They were not the last ones up when the call came the next morning — \"how! how!\"", "The fire was built up again, breakfast was started, and the Frenchmen went off to bring in the horses so we could get an early start.", "Harry and Josette did their parts, under our direction, and the meal was a simple one. We sat down around the table-mat, each of us with a cup and a knife. We ate. We finished. No men came back. No horses came back. When another half-hour had gone by, my husband took Harry and went out to look for them himself.", "The day wore on. One after another they would come in to report progress, which meant no progress. Petaille and Lecuyer eventually turned up with two of the horses. The rest were not anywhere. Mr. Kinzie and Harry came back wet to the knees from the dew standing in the long prairie grass, with nothing to tell us. They ate, and went out again, and came back the same way.", "The rest of us had spent the whole morning at the encampment turning it over. Could the Indians have taken them? It was hardly possible. These people almost never committed a robbery in time of peace, and never against our family, whom they counted among their best friends. The horses would be found. They had probably been carelessly fastened the evening before and had simply wandered farther than they usually did.", "We held a council and decided to send Grignon back to Chicago to get fresh horses from Gholson Kercheval and return as fast as he could. If he found the encampment empty when he got back, he was to understand that we had recovered our own animals and gone on to Fox River, where he would certainly catch us.", "He had been gone less than an hour when the missing horses came hopping slowly out of a point of woods to the north of us — a stand of trees that every one of the searchers swore he had gone over again and again — and made straight for the spot where we were sitting. They came up as demurely as though nothing at all had happened. They seemed genuinely surprised to be met with a hearty scolding instead of the usual patting and praise.", "It took a short half-hour to strike and pack the tent, stow the mats and the kettles, saddle up, and get mounted for the road."],
  p3s4: ["\"Whoever pleases may take my place in the carriage,\" our mother said. \"I have traveled so many years on horseback, that I find any other mode of conveyance too fatiguing.\"", "And then, at sixty years of age, she went up onto sister Margaret's pacer with the ease of a girl of sixteen.", "Lieutenant Foster had gone from us early that morning — he had to rejoin his command — and now the Sau-ga-nash, having seen us ready to start with a clear sky over us and everything right and tight for the trip, said his good-byes and turned back toward Chicago.", "We rode on through a beautiful country, glade and forest by turns, until we came to the Fox River. The current ran clear over its bed, rippling as it went, and as we started down the steep bank toward the water the question came up that always comes up in a country you do not know. Is it fordable?", "Petaille knew this ground. Petaille had not yet caught up to us. Lecuyer knew nothing whatever about it. The troops had plainly gone ahead of us by this same trail — true enough, but the troops had been on horseback. The thing in doubt was whether we could get the carriage over. And understand, the doubt was never about how deep the water was. It was about what lay underneath it, and whether that would hold.", "We agreed that two or three riders should try it first. My mother, Lecuyer, and I went across slowly, each of us picking a different place to come out on the far bank, so as to find the firmest footing. The bottom was hard and good almost the whole way. Near the shore it gave a little under the hooves. Then one more step and each of us stood on dry ground.", "\"Est-il beau?\" my husband called. He was driving.", "\"Oui, monsieur.\"", "\"Yes, John, come just here, it is perfectly good.\"", "\"No, no — go a little farther down. See the white gravel just there — it will be firmer still, there.\"", "That was the sort of direction he was getting, all of it at once and none of it agreeing. He took the second. One step short of the bank, both horses went down until there was hardly more of them showing than their backs.", "The white gravel was not gravel. It was a bed of yellow clay, and the water lying over it had made it gleam like something solid.", "The horses were half suffocated with mud and water and they fought the harness in a frenzy. My husband went out of the seat and onto the pole. \"Some one give me a knife,\" he shouted. I was in the water again at once, and I went as close as I dared and put mine into his hand, out of the scabbard I wore around my neck.", "\"Whatever you do, don't cut the traces,\" his mother cried.", "He cut through some of the side-straps. He had worked his way out to the very end of the pole and was reaching forward to get at the head-couplings when one horse gave a wild plunge and the other reared and went over almost onto its back. My husband was between them. For a moment we thought he was gone, trampled down under them. Then he came up out of it, so covered in mud and water that we could barely make him out. Harry was near him, and Harry went forward along the pole with the quickness of a cat, and with the sharp jack-knife he already had open he cut the straps that held their heads.", "By then both horses were lying floating on the surface, one of them to all appearances dead and the other gasping what looked like its last breath. But the instant they felt their heads come free, both of them made another furious effort together to get loose from the pole, which still held them by the neck-strap.", "That failed, so they tried something else. With a few well-judged twists and turns they wrenched the pole apart, and finally carried it off across the river in triumph and up the bank, where they stood together and waited to see what we intended to do next.", "Here was a predicament. A few hours earlier we had thought ourselves badly used because a few of our horses had gone missing. This was worse. The wagon was standing in the river, the harness was cut to pieces and had then been carried off in the most independent manner by Tom and his companion, the pole was twisted into fragments, and there was not so much as a stick on our side of the water to make another one out of.", "Then a whoop came from the far bank, and two or three good ones went back to it from our party, and it was Petaille Grignon, come at last. He got down and took charge of the horses, who were standing under a shady tree resting after their exertions, and by that time Lecuyer had crossed over and the two of them brought the delinquents back.", "Meanwhile we had been doing what we could for our sister Margaret. She had been in the wagon when it went down, with her little son Edwin, and it had taken some work to get the two of them out and carried to shore on horseback. The shock of it brought on a fit of the ague, and she lay now in the long grass of the little prairie along the bank, blue and shaking. The tent had been packed at the back of the wagon and was too soaked with mud and river water to shelter anyone; all we could do was spread it in the sun to dry. We opened an umbrella over her head. Then we started on the question of repairs. First the wagon needed a new pole, which meant the master and the men had to recross the river and pick out an iron-tree in the forest.", "Then the harness. Someone had had the foresight to put a little box under the wagon seat with an awl in it, and waxed ends, and a number of other small things exactly suited to a situation like this one.", "It went question and answer, like Cock Robin:", "\"Who can mend the harness?\"", "\"I can, for I learned when I was a young girl to make shoes as an accomplishment, and I can surely now, as a matter of usefulness and duty, put all those wet, dirty pieces of leather together.\"", "So we all sat down on the grass in the shade of the only two umbrellas we could muster between us.", "I stitched away steadily at it. I will admit that I blistered my hands in no small degree.", "They had brought a suitable young tree back across, and the hatchets — nobody travels in the woods without them — were shaping it into a pole when a peculiar hissing noise started up, and the cry went up at once: \"Un serpent sonnette! A rattlesnake!\"", "Every one of us came to our feet, even the poor shaking invalid, and we were up just in time to see the thing slide past within three inches of my mother's feet, while the men fell on the place it had left with whips and missiles and anything else that would add to the general commotion.", "That little incident turned out to be an excellent cure for the ague. One excitement drives out another, and on that homoeopathic principle sister Margaret came along so well that by the time the day's mischiefs were all repaired she was ready to take her place in the cavalcade, as bright and cheerful as the rest of us."],
  p3s5: ["All of that had cost us so much of the day that our afternoon's ride came to very little. We got no farther than the shore of a beautiful sheet of water that people now call Crystal Lake. Its clear surface was covered over with loons, and with Poules d'Eau — a kind of rail that swarms in this region at certain seasons of the year.", "The Indians have the genius of Aesop for the character of animals. There is not a peculiarity in the look or the habits or the disposition of any creature that they do not have a fable ready to account for.", "The back of the little rail is deeply hollowed, scooped inward. The Indians say it came to be that way like this. Most of the Northwestern tribes hold that there is an invisible being among them, something on the order of the Genie in the stories of the East. Nan-nee-bo-zho is not exactly the father of evil. His office is to punish whatever is amiss, and he is described as forever busy trapping the animals that cross his path and making examples of them.", "One fine evening, walking along the bank of a lake, he saw a flock of ducks out on the blue water, sailing about and enjoying themselves. He called to them.", "\"Ho! come with me into my lodge, and I will teach you to dance!\" Some of the ducks said among themselves, \"It is Nan-nee-bo-zho, let us not go.\" Others thought differently. His words were fair and his voice was easy to listen to, and a few of them turned their faces toward the land. The rest followed soon after, and the whole flock came trooping up the bank behind him with a good deal of pleasant quacking, and went into his lodge.", "Once they were inside, the first thing he did was take up an Indian sack with a wide mouth and tie it by the strings around his neck, so that it hung down over his shoulders with the mouth of it left open. Then he stood himself in the center of the lodge and arranged the ducks in a circle around him.", "\"Now,\" he said, \"you must all shut your eyes tight, whoever opens his eyes at all, something dreadful will happen to him. I will take my Indian flute and play upon it, and you will, at the word I shall give, open your eyes, and begin dancing, as you see me do.\"", "The ducks did as they were told. They shut their eyes tight and kept the time by stepping from one foot to the other, all of them impatient for the dancing to start.", "Then there came a sound like a quack with something over it. None of them dared open their eyes.", "The flute kept breaking off. Each time it broke off there was that gurgling cry again — qu-a-a-ck. There was one little duck among them, much smaller than the others, and at about this point she could not stand it any longer and opened one eye, carefully. She saw Nan-nee-bo-zho playing his flute with one hand, and stooping down every so often to take hold of the duck nearest him, and throttle it, and push it into the bag on his shoulders. She edged herself a little way out of the circle, toward the door, which had been left partly open to let in the light, and then she called out — \"Open your eyes — Nan-nee-bo-zho is choking you all and putting you into his bag!\"", "And with that she flew. Nan-nee-bo-zho came down on her. His hand closed over her back, and she tore herself out of it by main force and got into the open air. Her companions came screaming and quacking out after her. Some of them escaped. Some of them stayed with the sprite.", "The little duck had saved her life and lost her beauty. She kept forever after the shape she had been forced into in that one moment — her back pressed down in the middle, her head and neck stretched out unnaturally into the air."],
  p3s6: ["The third day of our trip came up brilliantly clear, the same as the two before it, and we bent our course farther to the north than we had been going, toward Big-foot Lake — which people now call, with a good deal less imagination, the Lake of Geneva.", "That day nothing went wrong. Nothing at all. The air was soft, the foliage of the forest was fresh and gave off its scent as we passed through it, the little brooks ran clear and sparkling, and every part of it spoke the praises of the Creator who had been good to us.", "It is in country like this, a long way off from the noise and the striving and the sin of civilized places, that a person most completely feels the presence of the great Author of the Universe. Here the mind can adore his majesty and his goodness without anything standing in between. Here, and only here, is the command actually kept: Let all the earth keep silence before Him.", "And it can hardly be missed that the deepest and most solemn devotion lives in the hearts of those who are shut out from the worship of God in temples made with hands, and are driven instead to commune with him in the middle of the boundless magnificence that his own power framed.", "The day was not entirely without incident, either. We had stopped for our noon food and drink, and dismounted, and thrown ourselves down on the fresh herbage right at the edge of a pleasant thicket, when a tender bleating started up somewhere close to us. Then the low branches parted and a sweet little dappled fawn came through them and out among us, plainly hunting for its mother. It showed no fear of us whatever. Poor Selkirk's lines might be parodied for it: it was so unacquainted with man, its tameness was charming to us.", "Then the children began shouting with delight, and that sent the fawn bounding straight back into the woods, and any hope of catching it and keeping it for a pet ended right there.", "We had covered good ground that day and were beginning to feel it, when just before sunset we came up onto a ridge that looked down into one of the loveliest little dells I have ever seen. It was an oak opening. Under the shade of the tall scattered trees, the cattle and horses of the soldiers were browsing — they had gotten that far. Two or three white tents stood at the bottom of the valley beside a clear stream. The camp-fires were already lit, and the men were moving about them singly and in groups, seeing to their own comfort or their animals'.", "Lieutenant Foster came out to welcome us with great delight, and accepted without a moment's hesitation an invitation to join our mess again for as long as we should be traveling together.", "We found ourselves a pleasant encamping-ground, far enough off from the other party to spare us any inconvenience, and our supper was improved by a kettle of fine fresh milk brought over by Mrs. Gardiner, the hospital matron, who with her little covered cart was no unimportant feature of that military group. We ate our evening meal with a great deal of hilarity and enjoyment.", "If people are ever good company for one another, it is under circumstances like these. There has always been enough incident in the day to supply the talk and the laughing for as long as anyone is inclined to keep it up — which, to tell the truth, is not an unconscionable length of time after their supper is over.", "The poor Lieutenant looked grave enough when we set out ahead of him the next morning. Nobody in his party knew the road. Mr. Kinzie gave him directions, general and particular, and promised that at every place that looked more than usually doubtful he would blaze a tree or set up a chip to show the way."],
  p3s7: ["The country changed under us now, more varied than anything we had crossed so far — hills swelling up gently, lovely valleys between them, streams running bright at the bottom. What it did not have was living things. Now and then a shout from whoever was leading (we traveled Indian file, as the custom is) would turn all our heads toward a herd of deer loping, as the Westerners say it, off through the trees; or somebody would put a spur in at the sight of a small dark shape far ahead of us on the trail. It never came to anything. The game always contrived to be gone before we could get near it, and to leave the beaten track for a proper hunt was out of the question.", "A little after midday we came down a long sloping knoll, and at a sudden turn the whole lake opened in front of us — Gros-pied to the French, Maunk-suck to the people who lived on it, and Big-foot to us, after the chief whose village stood above its water. Bold hills pushed forward into the clear blue of it, or drew back a little to leave a green level nook where a man might set down a home. Along the nearer shore ran a bright gravelly beach, and here and there a small pure stream came coursing across it to join the larger water.", "On rising ground at the foot of one of the bluffs in the middle distance stood a cluster of neat wigwams, with gardens around them. They did not spoil the picture. They completed it.", "The whole party shouted out loud without meaning to. It was like the Hudson, only less bold — no, it was like the lake of the Forest Cantons in the picture of the Chapel of William Tell. What could be imagined more enchanting? Oh, if our friends at the East could only be here to enjoy it with us.", "We stayed a long while looking at it, and then put the spurs in and skirted the head of the lake, and before long we were climbing the broad platform where the village of Maunk-suck, or Big-foot, stood.", "They had watched us coming from a long way off. Every one of them was assembled in front of the wigwams by the time we arrived, ready to greet us if we were friends, and to do whatever the occasion should call for if we were not. Nothing like the sight of us had ever come up that rise before. Their welcome was every bit as warm as we had expected. \"Shaw-nee-aw-kee\" and his mother, who was known through the whole tribe by that touching name, \"Our friend's wife,\" were received with real kindness, and an animated talk started up that I could follow only as far as the gestures carried it. So I gave myself over to studying everything in front of me.", "The chief was a large man, raw-boned and ugly, his face swollen out of shape by drink, with something sinister and unpleasant in his expression. He had a gay-colored handkerchief tied over his head and was otherwise dressed in his best, out of compliment to the strangers.", "This was the chief to whom Chambly — Shaw-bee-nay, they call him now — and Billy Caldwell and Robinson were sent by Dr. Wolcott, their Agent, during the Winnebago war in 1821, to do everything in their power to keep him and his band from joining the hostile Indians. It took some doing, but they managed it, and by managing it they almost certainly saved the life of every settler then living exposed on the frontier.", "Of all the groups of his people standing around us, none held my eye like one young man. His face was handsome and his figure was striking even in a crowd where every figure was fine and well made. He too had a gay handkerchief on his head, and a shirt of the brightest lemon-colored calico, and silver ornaments in quantity, and — this was what gave the whole costume its strangeness — one legging of blue and the other of bright scarlet. I knew what that particular detail meant. It is the sign of a heart suffering under the tender passion. The flute in his hand said the same thing over again, and the joyous, animated look on his face said, just as plainly, that he was not a lover in any danger of despair.", "It was easy to picture him lately back from the chase, loaded with game, walking into the lodge of the fair one as the custom is and throwing his burden down at her parents' feet with a superb, indifferent air, as much as to say: here is some meat, it is a mere trifle, but it will show you what you might expect with me for a son-in-law. And I had no doubt at all that the girl had stepped forward and gathered it up, which is how she accepts the offering and the man who brought it. There was nothing in the look or the manner of any of the young women around us to show which of them was the happy one, and though I searched every face I could not find so much as a conscious blush anywhere. So I picked out the youngest and the prettiest of them for myself and went on weaving my romance to my own satisfaction.", "The village sat inside a ring of hills so steep, cut by gorges so narrow, that climbing out seemed barely possible on horseback. How we were to get a four-wheeled carriage up was the question now being argued between my husband and the Pottowattamies. There was no choice but to try it, by whichever pass the inhabitants judged the most practicable. Petaille went first and I came after him on my favorite Jerry. It was a scramble of a kind one does not often take — very nearly straight up, through what looked like the dry bed of a torrent, filled now with loose stones and hardly offering one secure foothold from the bottom to the top. I hung on to the mane, and part of the way I had both arms around Jerry's neck outright, and with the shouts and cheers of the people below coming up after us we got there — safe, and very nearly out of breath — and sat on the pinnacle looking down to see how the next party would fare.", "The horses had been taken out of the carriage. Its luggage went up on the shoulders of the young Indians, to be toted to the top. Then ropes were made fast to its sides and a whole crowd of our red friends took hold of them, with our two Frenchmen at the head, and two or three more got in behind to hold it and keep it from running backward. Then the work started. Such pulling. Such shouting. Such clapping of hands from the spectators of both sexes, and such stentorian orders and encouragement bawled out by the bourgeois. Every so often there would be a check, a wavering, the whole thing seeming about to go over backward into the plain below — and then no, they would have it again, and after efforts I would not have believed possible the carriage came safely up onto the table-land with the rest of us. In time everything and everyone was landed there. We paid our friends to their satisfaction, gathered up the goods and chattels, repacked the wagon, and set off for our encampment at Turtle Creek.", "Between the labor of that climb, the excitement of it, and a sun that kept getting hotter, that afternoon's ride was more uncomfortable than anything we had felt on the whole journey. We were truly glad when our guide's whoop went up and a few scattered lodges came into view and we knew we had reached our ground. We picked a beautiful sequestered spot beside a clear, sparkling stream, and when we had dismounted and seen the horses made comfortable, my husband gave his men their directions and then took me off to a private place where I could put aside my hat and my mask and bathe my flushed face and aching head in the cool water. I have never felt anything so grateful, so delicious. I sat down and leaned my head back against one of the tall trees overshadowing the place, and I was nearly dreaming when they called me in to the evening meal.", "The Indians had made us a present of some fine brook trout, and our Frenchmen had cooked them in the most tempting way, and before the bright moon came up and we were ready for our rest, the headache and the fatigue had both gone out of me.", "One of the finest things about traveling this way is the joyous, vocal life of the forest at first light, when every feathered creature in it comes out to pay its cheerful salutation to the opening day."],
  p3s8: ["The bob-o'-link runs off his rapid chattering flourish; the thrush lays a soft whistle over it; the wood-dove puts in her tender coo, and the grouse a deep warbling bass, and the partridge his drumming; then the lark's melodious trill, the robin's gay carol, and the friendly, familiar calling of the duck and the teal — and all of it coming at once out of the trees and the knolls and the low ground, until the simple half-breed says the only thing there is to say: \"Voilà la forêt qui parle!\" It seems as though a man could not help raising his own voice to take his part in the chorus — the mating song of praise.", "Birds and flowers and the soft balmy air of the morning. Must it not have been in a scene like this one that Milton's Adam poured out that beautiful hymn of adoration — These are thy glorious works, Parent of Good?", "Our hope for that day was to reach, early, the broad expanse of the Rock River that widens here into the Kosh-ko-nong. What that name means, in a language whose people's later fate makes it doubly affecting to say, is the lake we live on.", "For the first part of the day the road ran through forest so thick and tangled that Grignon and Lecuyer had to go out ahead of us as pioneers with their axes, cutting away the shrubs and branches that shut the way. It was slow work and at times it was discouraging. We got through it at last, and came out into country of an entirely different kind — low prairie, cut across by deep narrow streams as straight as canals, and every one of them a delay for horses and a real difficulty for carriages.", "Several times that forenoon the horses had to be taken out of the carriage and the carriage pulled and pushed over those deep narrow channels however it could be managed.", "Nobody has ever been happier to see the wooded banks of the Kosh-ko-nong than we were when they finally came up in front of us. Five or six miles of riding through beautiful oak openings brought us to Man-Eater's village — a cluster of neat bark wigwams with wide fields on either side of it, corn and beans and squashes lately planted and already promising a fine crop. In front of it lay the broad blue lake. To the south its shores were open and marshy; near the village, and away north as far as we could see, they were lined with fine tall trees. The village stood a little below the point where the Rock River opens into the lake, and while our party talked with the Indians there, an arrangement was made for them to take us across at a place about half a mile above.", "After a short halt we took up the line of march again through the woods, following the bank of the river.", "A number of the Winnebagoes went by water toward the appointed place, paddling the largest and strongest of their canoes — we had been among our own people ever since we left Gros-pied Lake.", "At the spot we dismounted and the men began unsaddling and unloading. We were put into the canoes first and paddled over to the far bank. Then the horses were swum across. Then the carriage. Two long wooden canoes were lashed firmly together side by side, wide enough between them for the carriage to stand upright, and the crossing began. Again and again those tottering hulls would lean, and a cry or a shout would go up from the party on shore as the whole mass looked to be going over sideways into the river — and then it would right itself, and after a good many departures from the perpendicular it came to shore safely.", "Now we thought our troubles were finished and there was nothing left to do but mount up and trot for Fort Winnebago as fast as we could go. No. Half a mile on there was a swamp. It was not wide, that much is true. It was two to three feet deep in mud and water, and whether we could get the carriage across it at all was an open question. Several of the Indians came along with us to that place, partly to lend their help and advice, and partly for the pleasure of watching what happened.", "When we got to it, we were inclined to laugh at all the formidable things that had been said about it. What we saw was a strip of somewhat low ground with tall dry rushes standing all over it.", "The ground did look a little wet. There was nothing there to justify the alarm anyone had been raising. So it was a considerable surprise to me when my husband, who had been gone from us a few minutes, came back into the circle wearing his duck trousers, with no shoes and no stockings on.", "\"What are you going to do?\" asked I.", "\"Carry you through the swamp on my shoulders. Come, Petaille, you are the strongest — you are to carry Madame Kinzie, and To-shun-nuck there,\" pointing to a tall, stout Winnebago, \"he will take Madame Helm.\"", "\"Wait a moment,\" I said, and I sat down on the grass and took off my own boots and stockings without hurrying about it.", "\"What is that for?\" they all asked.", "\"Because I don't wish to ride with wet feet all the rest of the day.\"", "\"No danger of that,\" they said, and not one of them did as I had done.", "By the time they were out in the middle of it, they had reason to think better of my precaution. The water our bearers had to wade was deep enough that nothing the ladies could do kept their feet above the surface of it, and I had the satisfaction of knowing that my weight on my husband's shoulders was far less trouble to him than it might have been, since I could hold the position I had started in instead of shifting about constantly to stay clear of the water.", "The laugh was entirely on my side when I put my boots and stockings back on and got up into my saddle dry-shod.", "It will be seen that traveling in the woods is, to some extent, a deranger of ceremony and formality, and that it necessarily cuts into our conventionalities. The only remedy for it is to take a double share of them when we come back to the civilized walks of life.", "By dint of a great deal of pulling and shouting and encouraging and threatening, the horses finally dragged the carriage through that ugly passage, and our red friends were free to go back to their village with what was certainly a very exaggerated and very amusing account of everything they had seen and had a hand in.", "We had not forgotten our promise to Lieutenant Foster about a guide-board of some kind, to help him follow us. Several times we carried pieces out of the woods with us, three or four feet long, and planted them at certain points along the way, with a stick set crosswise through a cleft in the top to show which direction he and his party were to take.", "We felt sure that, coming a few days behind us, he would find our trail and have the benefit of the same help we had had in getting through the hard places."],
  p3s9: ["That night we were to camp somewhere short of the Four Lakes. The heat had taken everything out of us, and the day's riding had taken the rest, and we watched the ground ahead for the low clumps of willow and alder that meant water was under them. None of us had another hour in us. We could have pushed on to the bank of the nearest lake, but the lake lay off our line, and off our line was a luxury we could not afford.", "It was late when we found a place that would do. We told each other, more than once, that this was the last night we would sleep on the road. Tomorrow we would be at Winnebago.", "The whole trip had been a pleasure — one long stretch of high spirits, and even the mishaps, which had been maddening while they lasted, had turned into things we laughed about afterward. Still, people are made the way they are made. We were ready to trade all that excitement for the quiet of being home again.", "The next morning's ride was calmer than any that had gone before it, because early in the day we came out onto what everyone called the Twenty-mile Prairie, and I will say here that miles on the prairie are longer than miles anywhere else. Crossing it was like crossing a desert with the sand taken out. Mile after mile of open ground, unbroken. Not a tree. Not one living thing in sight except ourselves.", "The sun seemed to be making up for two months of hiding, and it burned twice as hard for the arrears. You cannot carry an umbrella on horseback. The ones in the wagon could, and did.", "Our mother's strength had held her in the saddle every day until this one. On this one it simply ran out, and she gave up her place on little Brunet to sister Margaret.", "So we rode, one low knoll lifting past another, and from the top of each of them in turn we looked for the far line of timber that was, to us, the promised land.", "\"Take courage,\" came the encouraging word, again and again, \"very soon you will begin to see the timber.\"", "Another hour would drag itself past.", "\"Now, when we reach the rising ground just ahead, look sharp.\"", "We looked sharp. Nothing but the same landscape, as unvarying as before.", "There were not even streams out there to touch the thirst that fatigue and impatience had built in us.", "Then a whoop from Shaw-nee-aw-kee broke the silence we had been riding in.", "\"Le voilà!\" (There it is!)", "Our unpractised eyes could not find it at first — a faint blue strip laid along the horizon — and then we could, and it grew, and went on growing, and as it grew the fatigue and the thirst and the misery went out of us in exact proportion.", "We were in high spirits by the time we came in under Hastings's Woods, a fine, tall forest with a clear, quick stream running through it.", "The green shade and the cold water were food and drink to us, but we did not let ourselves forget that the day was moving on, and that we meant to be in before sunset. So we made ourselves get up and go, when another hour under those trees would have been the easiest thing in the world.", "The marsh at Duck Creek, after a week of that heat, was nothing like what it had been a few months earlier, when I had gone down into its ice water.", "We crossed it without trouble and came out on the bank of the creek itself.", "The stream was held to be fordable at that point, and in any case most of our party would not have cared much either way. To the ladies the question seemed to want a little more attention than that.", "\"This water looks very deep — are you sure we can cross it on horseback?\"", "\"Oh, yes! Petaille, go before, and let us see how the water is.\"", "Petaille went. He was mounted on a horse built like a giraffe, and by sticking his legs straight out to either side he got across with hardly a splash on him.", "The water was plainly going to come into the wagon. That mattered less, since at worst the passengers could climb up and stand on the seats.", "My horse, Jerry, was a good deal taller than average, so I was over quickly, with no trouble beyond having to pull my feet out of the stirrups and tuck them up tight against his mane.", "Sister Margaret was still on Brunet. Somebody said she had better change to one of the taller horses, and while that was being discussed the perverse little wretch settled it himself by walking into the creek after the others, without consulting anyone.", "He was out of his depth almost at once. We were frightened and delighted at the same moment, watching her draw her feet up out of the stirrups with astonishing quickness and perch herself on the very top of the saddle, where she stayed, and steered her small mutinous horse safely up onto the far bank.", "That was the last of our adventures. Four easy miles brought us to the Fort with the sun laying its last light across the whole glowing country, and at the ferry the friends who had been waiting for us took us straight up to the hospitable roof of Major Twiggs."],
  p3s10: ["The companies of the First Infantry that had been holding Fort Winnebago had their orders before we ever rode in. They were to move on to the Mississippi the moment a portion of the Fifth, then at Fort Howard, came up to relieve them.", "A good many of the officers of the Fifth had wives, which meant every set of quarters in the post would be spoken for. So although Major Twiggs pressed us hard to come back inside the Fort and stay until he went on furlough, we decided it was better to set ourselves up at once at \"the Agency.\"", "It was a very grand name for a very small concern, and I could never say it without wanting to laugh. The heads of department at Washington had promised us a comfortable house as soon as Congress voted money enough to cover any extra expense in the Indian Department. Congress, it was clear, bore us some private grudge, because it had let two whole sessions go by without troubling itself about where we slept. There was nothing to be done about that. We would be comfortable on whatever we had.", "What we had were the old log barracks, thrown up two years before for the officers and men when the post was first established. Our French engagés had taken them apart and stood them up again on the little hill across from the Fort. To these were now added a dairy, stables, a smoke-house and the rest, built of tamarack logs hauled out of the swamp nearby. The whole arrangement looked exactly as rough and as makeshift as it was.", "The main building was a row of four rooms. No two of them opened into each other. Each had its own door onto the open air, and a small window cut straight through the logs front and back to let in light. An enormous clay chimney served every two rooms and took up one whole side of each. Overhead, where a ceiling should have been, there were a few rough boards laid across the transverse logs that carried the roof.", "It was surprising how quickly those old broken-down rooms began to feel like a home — a few Indian mats down on the floor, the piano and the rest of the furniture set where furniture belongs, even some pictures hung against the logs. The pictures did not last. With the first hard rain the water found its way in through any number of crevices, and after that we made a habit of putting away anything that moisture could ruin. We laughed about it. We also packed the carpets and the extra furniture up onto the boards overhead, which we grandly called the attic, and settled down to wait for the day the Government would condescend to remember that we existed. The thing that wore on me most was the straw bonnet. I had to wear it all day long, crossing from bedroom to parlor and from parlor to kitchen, and I grew so used to having it on that I would sometimes forget to take it off when I sat down to the table, or to some quiet piece of work with my mother and sister."],
  p3s11: ["Permission did come, in time, to build a house for the blacksmith — meaning the man the Government kept in pay at this station to mend the guns and traps of the Indians.", "It happened to be the luckiest thing possible for us that Monsieur Isidore Morrin was a bachelor, and perfectly content to go on boarding with his friend Louis Frum, dit Manaigre. So once the new house was genuinely under way, we laid it out and drove it forward strictly on our own account.", "It was no palace. A parlor and two bedrooms on the ground floor, two low chambers up under the roof, a kitchen at the back. Set beside the rambling stable of a building we were living in then, it might as well have been one.", "Before it was finished, word reached Mr. Kinzie that the money for the annual Indian payment was waiting for him in Detroit — his to take charge of, his to see safely carried to the Portage — and he had to leave at once.", "The workmen brought up from the Mississippi to put up the main building were good enough to go on without anyone standing over them. The kitchen, though, was the Frenchmen's job, and nobody could say how that would get done with the bourgeois gone.", "\"You will have to content yourselves in the old quarters until my return,\" said my husband, \"and then we will soon have things in order.\"", "His trip would be a long, slow business. The Government did not move by railroad and telegraph in those days.", "After he was gone I said to the men, \"Come, you have all your logs cut and hauled — the squaws have brought the bark for the roof — what is to prevent our finishing the house and getting all moved and settled to surprise Monsieur John on his return?\"", "\"Ah! to be sure, Madame John,\" said Plante, who always did the talking, \"provided the one who plants a green bough on the chimney-top is to have a treat.\"", "\"Certainly. All hands fall to work, and see who will win the treat.\"", "On the strength of a prize for whoever laid the last stroke on the building, Plante, Pillon, and Manaigre — whom the waggish Plante insisted on calling \"mon nègre,\" always at a moment when he was safely out of arm's reach — went at it hard.", "Building a log house is a strange process to watch. The logs go up one on another, jointed at the corners, until the walls stand as high as they are meant to stand. The chimney starts as four poles of the right length, laced together with a wicker-work of small branches. Someone digs a pit close by and works clay and water into a kind of mortar. Big wisps of hay are loaded with the thick stuff and shaped by hand into what are called, in the trade, clay cats, and these get packed into the frame of the chimney until there is not a chink left anywhere in it. Then the whole thing is smoothed over with a coat of wet clay, and that is what they call plastering.", "Between the logs of the walls, small bits of wood are driven in close together — that is chinking — and once it is done the clay cats go in after them and are smoothed down with the plaster. When it has all dried, the walls and the chimney are whitewashed, and the result looks tidy and comfortable.", "The roof is thick sheets of bark laid over the transverse logs. Around the chimney, where rain does its worst, we were careful to have a few layers of palisade put down — the ones left over when Mr. Peach, an odd little itinerant genius, had fenced in our garden, which was the pride and the wonder of the settlement and the wigwams around us."],
  p3s12: ["All the while this work was going on, our Indian friends kept coming to see us. First among them, and foremost, was the young Dandy — Four-Legs.", "He turned up one fine morning with two women, whom he presented as his wives. He had a little Chippewa, and on that thin bridge he and our mother contrived to hold something like a conversation. He had come in everything he owned: brooches, wampum, fan, looking-glass, the whole inventory. The paint on his face and across his chest had plainly taken him a long while to get right.", "He took a chair, the way he had seen it done in Washington, and signed to his women to sit down on the floor.", "Taking two wives is not a common thing among the Indians. They appear to have worked out that the fewer of them a man has to manage, the more peace there is in the wigwam.", "Still, it happens now and then that a husband gets a foolish notion about a second woman, and then he has to spend every scrap of cunning and eloquence he owns bringing the first one around to the idea of another body in the lodge. It has to be handled delicately, or there will be no harmony in that house again.", "\"My dear, your health isn't very good, it is time you should have some rest. You have worked very hard, and it grieves me that you should have to labor any longer. Let me get you some nice young squaw to wait upon you, that you may live at ease all the rest of your life.\"", "The first wife agrees. She has no choice about it. And if she happens to be a jealous woman with a long memory, the life the newcomer leads after that does not bear describing. The old wife keeps every right she has as dowager and duenna, and the husband's tenderness is thin payment for what the young rival is put through.", "The Dandy came to us on a Sunday morning. We were all sitting quietly, reading. Four-Legs asked my mother why we were doing that, and why everything around us was so still.", "My mother explained the day of rest to him — that we set it aside for worshipping and serving the Great Spirit, as he had commanded in his Holy Word.", "Four-Legs nodded his approval. That was quite right, he said. He was glad to see us doing our duty. He was very religious himself, and he liked to see it in other people. He always saw to it that his squaws attended to their duties too — not reading, perhaps, but the sort of thing the Great Spirit liked, and that he considered proper and becoming.", "He had no appetite at all for hearing where our beliefs and his parted company. The conviction among the Winnebagoes that if the Great Spirit had wanted them any different from what they are, he would have made them so, is too solid a thing for argument or persuasion to move.", "Sometimes a man who wants to appear a little civilized will sit through everything you have to say about Christianity, and then remark pleasantly, \"Yes, we believe that too,\" and turn the talk to something else.", "As a rule they do not see what there is to be gained by taking up the religion and the habits of the whites. \"Look at them,\" they say, \"always toiling and striving — always wearing a brow of care — shut up in houses — afraid of the wind and the rain — suffering when they are deprived of the comforts of life! We, on the contrary, live a life of freedom and happiness. We hunt and fish, and pass our time pleasantly in the open woods and prairies. If we are hungry, we take some game, or, if we don't find that, we can go without. If our enemies trouble us, we can kill them, and there is no more said about it. What should we gain by changing ourselves into white men?\""],
  p3s13: ["Whatever the Christian missionaries had done, and they had worked hard at it, they had made very little headway in lighting up anybody's mind on the doctrines of the Gospel. While we were living at the Portage, Mr. Mazzuchelli, a Roman Catholic priest, came up on a missionary visit with Miss Elizabeth Grignon along as interpreter, and after some instruction from him about forty of them agreed to be baptized. They were given Christian names, which pleased them very much, and little plated crucifixes, which pleased them more. The women wore them around their necks. There was real devotion in the way they handled those crosses, but I had not enough of the language to learn from any of them whether they understood the first thing about what the symbol was meant to carry. This much is certain. Not one of them asked to learn our language so as to know the Saviour better, and not one of them showed the least anxiety to be told anything more about him than the missionary had managed to say in his short stay.", "One of the women, who had been given the name Charlotte, said she wanted to learn how white women kept house, and asked me, through Madame Paquette, whether she might come on washing-day and learn our way of doing the business from my servants. We gave her a tub, and my woman showed her by signs and example what she was to do. I was more than a little curious to see how it was going, so after a while I went out to the kitchen where they all were. Charlotte was standing at her tub, scouring and rubbing away with everything in her at her little crucifix. Two other women sat on the floor beside her, watching her do it.", "\"That is the work she has been at for the last half-hour,\" said Josette, out of all patience. \"She'll never learn to wash.\"", "Charlotte did then fall to in earnest, and went at the washing so hard it looked as though she meant to pull her own arms out of their sockets.", "After a time I decided she must be worn out with work she was not used to, and I did what everyone did in those days — what I had always seen done at home on washing-day, what I imagine was simply what housekeepers did. I went to the dining-room closet meaning to bring her a glass of wine, or brandy and water. My cupboard turned out to be in the condition of poor Mother Hubbard's. There was nothing of the kind in it anywhere, only a bottle of orange shrub.", "I poured out a wineglassful of that and carried it to her. She took it with obvious pleasure. Then, with it already on the way to her lips, she stopped. She said one word — \"Whiskey!\" — and handed it straight back to me. I would have pressed it on her even so, because in my ignorance I honestly believed a cordial was what she needed. She pointed to her crucifix, shook her head, and went back to her tub.", "I took that as a lesson worth more than twenty sermons. It was the first time in my life I had seen anyone refuse spirituous liquor on a religious principle, and it left a mark on me that I have never lost."],
  p3s14: ["Of all the women of the tribe we came to know in those first months, our favorite was a daughter of one of the Day-kau-rays. That family, as I have said elsewhere, claimed a cross of French blood somewhere back up the line, which may explain her fair skin and the soft curl in her hair. She had a fine broad forehead, full expressive eyes, good teeth. She had not gone brown and haggard with age the way the women of her people usually did. Except for one feature, you would have called her beautiful.", "Years before, she had married a Mus-qua-kee, a Fox Indian, and by the custom that holds among all the tribes it was the husband who came home to the wife's family, so he had been living among the Winnebagoes.", "It is this custom, the exact reverse of ours, that makes the birth of a daughter something to be congratulated on in an Indian family. She will bring another hunter to our lodge — that is how they say it to one another.", "The Mus-qua-kee stayed on among his wife's relations for a few years. No children came, and after a while he had had enough of these new people of his and wanted to go back to his own. For a time he worked on his wife to leave her home and go with him to the Mississippi, where the Sauks and Foxes lived. She would not. She could not make herself do it.", "One day, after a great many attempts that came to nothing, he lost his temper.", "\"Then, if you won't go with me,\" he said, \"I will leave you, but you shall never be the wife of any other man — I will mark you!\"", "And then he was on her, and he bit off the end of her nose. That is the usual punishment for infidelity in a wife, and it is the worst disgrace she can carry, because it shuts her out of marriage for the rest of her life. He ran off to his own people afterward. His revenge did not take, though. Day-kau-ray was too widely known and too thoroughly respected for any shame to stick to a member of his family, and this bright, warm creature in particular won everyone she met on the first meeting. She certainly had us from the start.", "She suffered badly from rheumatism, and a remedy we gave her relieved her almost entirely, after which her gratitude had no bottom to it. Long pain had left her partly crippled, but that did not stop her walking the whole ten miles from the Barribault as often as once every two or three weeks just to see us. Then she would sit and look at us, and laugh out loud with a child's delight at anything new or strange we happened to be doing, and pat us and stroke us whenever we came within reach, and sometimes lift one of our hands or arms and kiss it. That was how she showed love. We loved her back. It was always a happy shout in that house when somebody looking out along the Portage road called, \"The Cut-Nose is coming!\" In time, though, we learned to call her by her baptismal name, Elizabeth, because she too was one of Mr. Mazzuchelli's converts."],
  p3s15: ["She came one day with a half-grown boy behind her, and the boy was carrying a young fawn she had brought me for a present. I was charmed with it — the soft eyes, the dappled coat — but I had heard people say \"as wild as a fawn\" all my life and I did not expect to get anywhere with taming it. To my complete surprise, it learned in no time to follow me like a dog. Wherever I went, Fan went. At breakfast she would lie down under the table across my feet. One of her earliest gestures of affection was to chew every bit of trimming off my black silk apron while pretending to nuzzle and fuss over me. That was not the only kind of mischief in her, either.", "One day there was a great clattering of crockery from the kitchen. We ran in to see what had happened and found that Miss Fan had climbed onto a shelf of the dresser, about two feet off the floor, and was working out how to lie down comfortably among the plates and dishes. It did not take me long to see that what she wanted was the shelf above her head — that she always looked for a place to sleep under a chair, or under anything that made a roof of sorts over her. So after that, when her morning nap was coming on, I would open a big green parasol and stand it on the matting in the corner, and when I called \"Fan, Fan,\" she would come and settle in under it and go straight to sleep.", "One morning Fan was gone. We called for her and hunted the garden, the cattle enclosure, the Frenchmen's houses, the whole length of the hill toward Paquette's. No Fan anywhere. We decided her own wild nature had finally spoken and she had gone off to the woods.", "It was a hot forenoon and every door in the house stood open. Around dinner-time Fan came in at a dead run, panting so hard her whole body worked with it, and threw herself down on her side with her legs stretched straight out and her mouth foaming, and everything about her said she was dying. We tried to give her water. We tried to gentle her, in case it was only fright that had her like that. In a few minutes she gave a gasp, and a spasm went through her, and she was dead. Whether the greyhounds had run her, or whether she had eaten some poisonous weed and the pain of it had sent her back to the people she trusted most, we never found out. We only knew we had lost our pretty pet, and a good many tears were shed over her."],
  p3s16: ["Not long at all after my husband rode out, we had a visit from the White Crow, the Little Priest, and several more of the principal chiefs of the Rock River Indians. It plainly threw them to learn that their Father was away from home, even when they were told his errand was to go and get the silver. We sent for Paquette, and he interpreted for us what they had come about.", "They had come to tell us that Black Hawk, the Sauk chief, and his band — who under an earlier treaty had gone across and settled west of the Mississippi some time before — were back on their old homes and hunting-grounds. They had said plainly that they did not intend to give them up again, and that they meant to drive out the white settlers who had begun moving onto them.", "The driving off, in fact, the chief had already done. It was said he had brought some of the Pottowattamies over to him, and there was reason to be afraid he could talk some of the Winnebagoes into doing the same.", "The chiefs had come to take counsel with their Father, and to give him their word that they would do all they could to keep their young men quiet. They had heard troops were being raised among the whites down in Illinois, and they hoped their own people would have the sense to stay clear of the trouble. And they asked that their Father, when he came back, would see to it that the soldiers did not meddle with them, as long as they kept quiet and behaved as friends.", "White Crow seemed particularly set on making me understand one thing: that if any danger came while Shaw-nee-aw-kee was gone, he would come with his people and protect me and my family. I believed him. He had never in anything shown himself other than an upright and honorable man.", "Even so. Indian troubles that close, and our guardian and protector away from us, gave us a great many anxious moments. It was not until word came that the Sauks and Foxes had gone back peaceably west of the Mississippi that we could put the fear down altogether."],
  p3s17: ["Then came the parting with Major Twiggs and his family, and I could not manage it lightly. The Major had a few words for me before we went, one final report on our old acquaintance, Krissman.", "\"When I went into the barracks the other day,\" he said, \"about the time the men were taking their dinner, I noticed a great six-foot soldier standing against the window-frame, crying and blubbering. 'Halloo,' I said, 'what on earth does this mean?'\"", "\"'Why, that fellow there,' said Krissman (for it was he), 'has scrowged me out of my place!' A pretty soldier your protége will make, madam!\" added the Major.", "That was the last word I ever had of my hero. Perhaps he went off to display his prowess against the Seminoles and the Mexicans. Perhaps he went back to the fat soil of his native German Flats, and to the boatman's horn he so loved to blow. Some future historian will have to settle it.", "One person is still to be accounted for: Louisa. A chance offered in the spring, and the Major put her into the keeping of a traveller bound for Buffalo, to be delivered home to her parents. She left in style. Out of compliment to the new acquaintances she had formed, she cut her skirts short and drew on a pair of scarlet leggings worked over with porcupine-quills, and so made her farewell to military life. Into the gentleman's hands she also placed sixty dollars for safe keeping, money she said \"she had saved up, out of her wages at a dollar a week, through the winter.\""],
  p3s18: ["We were barely settled in the new house at the Agency before we tried starting a little Sunday-school. Edwin, Harry and Josette were the scholars we could count on, but there were also the two small Manaigres, Thérèse Paquette, and Thérèse's mother's half-sister, Florence Courville, a pretty girl of fifteen. Not one of the girls had ever learned her letters. They spoke only French, or rather the Canadian patois, and it was terribly hard work to give them the sound of a word and its meaning at the same time, and they always wanted the meaning. On top of that there was the business of undoing false ideas, and of clearing away the ignorance and superstition that stood in the road of any sensible improvement. We did what we could, and we had the satisfaction of seeing them, after a while, get on quite respectably with the spelling-book — and, better than that, come by a little light and knowledge about better things.", "In time, though, Florence began to be missing from her class. Her sister, she said, could not always spare her. She wanted her to keep house while she herself went over on Sunday to visit her friends the Roys, who lived on the Wisconsin.", "We took the matter up with Madame Paquette. Could she not spare Florence for one hour of the day? We would gladly teach her on a week-day, since she seemed anxious to learn — but we had always been told that for that there was no time.", "\"Well — she would see. Madame Alum\" — she meant Helm — \"and Madame John were so kind!\"", "The regularity never improved. After a while Manaigre was persuaded to send his children down to Mr. Cadle's mission-school at Green Bay. Thérèse went with them, and very soon after that Florence stopped coming at all.", "From then on we had to keep our teaching inside our own household."],
  p3s19: ["Long before we had any business expecting my husband back, a message came down asking me to come up to the new house. We all went together. We had kept away on purpose for a few days, waiting for exactly that summons, and we knew what it meant.", "Plante was up on the roof beside the kitchen chimney, sitting astride a small keg and enormously pleased with himself. On the very top of the chimney he had planted a green bough. He held on to it with one hand and waved the other at us and shouted, \"Eh bien, Madame John! à cette heure, pour le régal!\"", "\"Yes, Plante, you are entitled to a treat, and I hope you won't enjoy it the less that Pillon and Manaigre are to share it with you.\"", "A suitable reward left all three of them perfectly satisfied with their bourgeoise — against whom Plante had been known to grumble, on the grounds, he said, that she had him called up too early in the morning. He might have added that she could not follow the philosophy by which he came in to work in his own garden on the plea that it was too rainy to work in Monsieur John's.", "It was no ordinary feeling with which we walked out of the old log tenement and took possession of the new house, small and unimportant a thing as it was.", "I was only too glad of the luxury of a real bedchamber after more than two months of sleeping on the parlor floor. Our cooking arrangements, I admit, had not risen much. The clay chimney was not strong enough to carry the trammel and pot-hooks, which in those days had not yet been shoved aside by the cooking-stove and the kitchen-range. We built the fire the old way — great logs at the back, smaller sticks in front laid across the andirons — and any pot that would not fit on the hearth was set up on those sticks. But let one treacherous stick burn through while nobody was watching it, and down came the whole arrangement: kettles, saucepans, burning brands and cinders in one mass you could hardly separate. How often that happened while Harry or little Josette had charge of it, and the mistress of the house was in the parlor playing lady to a visitor, it were vain to tell.", "Then, for all of Monsieur Plante's palisades around the chimney, a hard shower would come pelting down, and the hearth sloped just enough the wrong way that the fire would drown. The bark on the roof did not always do its duty either, and now and then we were so thoroughly rained on indoors that there was nothing for it but to snatch up the breakfast or the dinner and tuck it away under the table until better times — that is, until it was fair again. In spite of every one of these small hostilities, we liked our new quarters enormously.", "The garden was doing well and full of vegetables, and even the currant bushes we had carried up from Chicago, tied in a bundle on the back of the carriage, had given us a little fruit.", "The Indian women came steadily, and they never came without presents. Sometimes venison, sometimes ducks or pigeons, whortleberries, wild plums or cranberries as the season turned, neat pretty mats for the floor or the table, wooden bowls and ladles, fancy work in deer-skin or porcupine-quills. They would carry these in and drop them at my feet. If I was distracted and failed to look pleased, and to lift the things up off the floor and put them carefully away, there would be a look of real hurt, and the remark, \"Our mother hates our gifts,\" and I would know exactly how much I had wounded them. A present had to be received graciously, and it had to be returned with something worth twice as much.", "Meanwhile the weeks went by, one after another, and still the master did not come.", "Schooners reached Green Bay so seldom that no one could ever say how long a passage across the lakes and back would take, and it was the end of August before he came home. He had not expected to find us moved and settled in and doing nicely, and it astonished him. With him there to drive it, the troubles we had been complaining of were put right in short order.", "At Fort Gratiot he had met my young brother Julian, and brought him along — my parents were sending him out, at our asking, to live with us. Edwin was overjoyed to have a companion again, having been very much alone until then. The boys had plenty to look at soon enough, because the summons had gone out to the villages, and the Indians began coming in for the payment."],
  p3s20: ["There was one among them that year I had never laid eyes on before — the mother of the elder Day-kau-ray. Nobody could tell you her age. Everyone agreed she had seen upwards of a hundred winters. Her eyes had gone dim and nearly white. Her face was dark and shrunken in on itself like a baked apple. Her voice shook and had almost nothing left in it, except when she raised it in fury at her graceless grandsons, who liked to play every kind of trick on her. All of it said the same thing about how long she had been alive.", "She went about on all fours, having no strength left to hold herself upright. On the day of the payment, once she had her portion and had hidden it away in a corner of her blanket, she came crawling along and settled herself on the door-step to count what she had.", "My sister and I were watching her from the open window.", "She had just spread the silver out in front of her, unobserved, as she believed, when two of her descendants came down on her out of nowhere. At first they seemed to be begging a share of it. She drove them off with angry gestures. Then one of them made a fast swoop with his hand and came away with a fistful.", "She tried to get up and go after him and could do nothing but clutch what was left and let out screams of rage that did not sound like anything human. Just then the boys happened to look up and see us watching them. They burst out laughing, and with a sort of mocking flourish they threw the half-dollars back to her and ran off to the pay-ground.", "For all their tormenting she was extremely fond of them, and she never came without begging something of her Father that she could turn around and give to them.", "She crept into the parlor one morning, then hauled herself straight up by the frame of the door and cried, in the most pitiful voice, \"Shaw-nee-aw-kee! Wau-tshob-ee-rah Thsoonsh-koo-nee-noh!\" (Silver-man, I have no looking-glass.) My husband, smiling, picked up the same small tone and called back, \"Do you wish to look at yourself, mother?\"", "She found this so irresistibly funny that she laughed until she had to sit herself down on the floor and give herself over to it. Then she admitted the mirror was for one of the boys. When her Father had handed it over, she discovered that she had no comb. Then that she had no knife. Then that she had no calico shawl — until it finished the way it always finished, with Shaw-nee-aw-kee paying pretty dearly for his joke."],
  p3s21: ["Watching the Indians come in, and watching them go out again, was a standing outrage to my sense of woman's rights. As a rule the master of the family came along at his leisure, carrying his gun and perhaps a lance. The woman came behind him with the mats and the poles of the whole lodge on her shoulders, her pappoose if she had one, her kettles, her sacks of corn and wild rice, and not seldom the family dog riding on the top of the load. If there happened to be a horse or a pony among the family's possessions, the man rode it and the squaw walked after.", "This lopsided division of the work does not come from any lack of kind or affectionate feeling in the husband. It is more the instinct of the sex to assert its superior standing and importance whenever a decent opportunity to do it comes along. Away from anybody's eyes, where there is no danger to his dignity, the husband is willing enough to take some of the burden custom puts on his wife, and to carry a share of her labors and her hardships himself.", "The payment had not gone by without its proper number of complimentary dances and medicine dances. The medicine dance comes only at long intervals. The complimentary dance comes whenever an occasion turns up that calls for a show of respect and courtesy.", "The custom is to go first to the person to be complimented and ask leave to dance for him. Leave granted, the preparation begins: the face painted with great care, the body marked — it is generally bare across the chest and shoulders — after whatever pattern is most approved. Every ornament that can be got hold of goes into the hair or the headdress. And happy is the man who, having taken a scalp or more than one, has the right to announce it with the matching number of eagle's feathers.", "The less fortunate make do with the feathers of a wild turkey, or, better yet, of the first unlucky rooster that crosses their path. My poor fowls, all through the time of the payment, were plucked absolutely bare.", "When they are ready, the dancers gather at some convenient place and come marching to the appointed ground with the Indian drum and the shee-shee-qua, or rattle, going. They form a circle and dance with violent contortions and gestures, some of them graceful and some of them only strenuous. The squaws stand a little off to one side and put their own harsh voices in among the instruments, and they rarely dance themselves. Sometimes, though, when the general gaiety has got into them, a few will make a circle outside the men and do a kind of ungraceful up-and-down movement whose only merit is its perfect time — and the Indians, without exception, seem born with an ear for time. The dance ends only when the dancers have nothing left in them. Then a quantity of presents are brought and piled in the middle of the circle by order of the person who was complimented. One of their own number divides it all out fairly, and, the whole purpose of the display having been served, they go.", "The medicine dance is held mainly to celebrate the Medicine-man's skill in curing disease. He belongs to a fraternity credited with the further power of interpreting the will of the Great Spirit concerning the conduct of his people. He sometimes makes offerings and sacrifices that are understood to be propitiatory, and in that sense the word priest fits him. He is a prophet too, so far as he is in a limited way a teacher — but he makes no claim to foretell what is coming.", "A man is picked for the fraternity of the Medicine-man by those already in it, and generally because they have noticed some skill or shrewdness in him. Sometimes a man who has been dangerously ill and has recovered under the treatment of one of the members is judged a fit choice, on the strength of the claim that recovery establishes.", "When he is to be initiated, there is a great feast, paid for, naturally, by the candidate himself, because in plain life as in civilized life the same rule of politics holds: honors must be paid for. An animal is killed and dressed and the people at large eat of it, and there are dances and songs and speeches without end. Then the chief Medicine-man takes the candidate aside and instructs him privately in all the ceremonies and all the knowledge that make a member complete. Now and then the new member chosen is still a child. In that case the Medicine-man takes him as soon as he is old enough and brings him up by instruction and example into a creditable member of the fraternity.", "There is a good deal of belief in magic among the Winnebagoes. Every Medicine-man has a bag or sack that is understood to have some animal shut inside it, and in the course of his pow-wows he speaks to that animal, calling out in the cry proper to the species he supposes it to be. The people appear entirely satisfied that the answers he announces are real communications, in that form, from the Great Spirit.", "The Indians seem to have no notion of any reckoning beyond this life. They hold hard to the great fundamental virtues of natural religion — the worship of the Great Spirit, brotherly love, love of one's children, honesty, temperance, chastity. Any breach of the Great Spirit's laws, by a falling away from those virtues, they believe will rouse his anger and bring punishment down. Those are their principles. That their practice shows a wider and wider departure from them, under the debasing influence of living close to the whites, is a melancholy truth, and no one will admit it with so much sorrow as the people who lived among them, and thought well of them, before that change set in.", "One of the first improvements that occurred to us about the new house was getting rid of some very ugly pickets standing around two or three Indian graves on the esplanade out in front. Burial-places are held in such reverence, though, that we understood we would have to come at the subject with a great deal of delicacy and care.", "My husband did finally venture to propose to Mrs. Pawnee Blanc, the nearest living relative of the person buried there, that the pickets be replaced with a neat wooden platform.", "The idea pleased her very much, since through her closeness to Paquette's family she had picked up something of a taste for civilization. So a little platform about a foot high, properly finished off with a moulding around the edge, went in where the worn black pickets had been, and it was a touching thing to see the mournful satisfaction with which two or three old women came every evening at sunset, and sat there gossiping over the ashes of their dead.", "On fine moonlit nights, too, you would often see a group of them sitting there in what is to them a solemn hour, for they hold the poetic belief that the moon was made to give light to the dead.", "The reverence the Indians pay to the memory of their departed friends, and the faithfulness with which they visit their graves and make offerings there to the Great Spirit, is an example their more enlightened brethren would do well to copy. Some of their other customs regarding the dead are another matter."],
  p3s22: ["When one of their number dies, the word goes round and the whole camp takes up the mourning and the lamentation. It is also, in some cases — and I am sorry to have to set this down — the excuse for a general carouse, wherever the means and appliances for one can be laid hold of.", "The relatives weep and howl in their grief, and the friends and acquaintances sit with them and keep them company out of sympathy. A few of them are then chosen to go to their Father, carry him the news, and ask him for some presents \"to help them,\" as they put it, \"dry up their tears.\"", "One morning, not long after the payment was concluded, that delegation came to us.", "The dead man was a drunken little Indian whom the French people around the place called \"Old Boilvin,\" because he looked like the Indian Agent of that name down at Prairie du Chien, and it was his death the application rested on. \"He had been fishing,\" they said, \"on the shores of one of the little lakes near the Portage, and, having taken a little too much ' whiskee,' had fallen into the water and been drowned.\" Nothing of him had turned up but the blanket left on the bank, which ruled out any funeral ceremonies; it did not, however, rule out a great lamentation, and his friends were ready with one.", "Their Father handed over tobacco, knives, calico, and looking-glasses, measured against what he judged a reasonable quantity of grief for the loss of so worthless a vagabond, and they went away.", "Whiskey was forbidden in the most stringent terms, and a keg of it was still no trouble at all to come by from one or another of the traders who had not yet gone. With the keg, and with the rest of their treasures, they gathered at an agreed spot not far from where the catastrophe had happened, set the keg down in the middle of them, and began being afflicted. The deeper they went into the keg, the louder their grief became, and the faster the tears ran.", "In the thick of all this a small figure came toward them, bent over and staggering, caked with mud, his clothes every which way, his face full of wonder and sympathy. He began, — \"Why? what? what? Who's dead?\"", "\"Who's dead?\" repeated they, looking up in astonishment. \"Why, you're dead! you were drowned in Swan Lake! Didn't we find your blanket there? Come, sit down and help us mourn.\"", "He did not need asking twice. He took a seat among them and cried and drank as the others were doing, mourning himself as bitterly as anyone there, and that strange scene ran on for precisely as long as there was whiskey left in the keg and breath enough in them to shape words."],
  p3s23: ["Whatever the tribe, the Indians love a story — telling one, or sitting still to hear one, and it makes no difference whether the thing is history or invention. Like the nations of the East they keep professional storytellers, men who travel from village to village and draw an audience that listens hard and admires, though what they recite is old and every soul in the lodge has heard it before.", "That is how the traditions come down whole, one generation handing them to the next with nothing worn off them. Their grasp of the geography of their own country is astonishing in its exactness. I have watched a man sit in his lodge and draw the Northwestern States in the ashes with a finger — not the statistics of them but the shape of them, the lakes, the rivers, the mountains — setting them down accurately, naming the distances from one to the next in days of travel without once stopping to think, and carrying his drawing and his account of it southward as far as Kentucky and Tennessee.", "They do the same with a life. They keep not only the great events of it but the man's face, his build, the way he carried himself, his manners, and every small habit or oddity that made him himself and nobody else.", "The women prefer fiction, and some of their stories mingle the comic and the pitiable in a way I have met nowhere else. I set down two of them here as specimens. The Indian names in them come from the Ottawa — the \"Courte-Oreilles\" — though every tongue and dialect has its own telling of the same tales.", "A great many peculiarities are laid on this animal. He is said to share the jackal's habit of disturbing the dead in their graves, and to hear him bark at night fills the Indians with a superstitious dread, since they hold that his voice foretells calamity and death. They say too that his coat was once a single even reddish-brown all over, and that his legs turned black in the way the story tells.", "A certain village had a chief, and the chief had a beautiful daughter. It came into his head one day to give a feast, and every animal was to be invited to it. The messenger carried word to the red fox, who wanted to know first, \"What are you going to have for supper?\"", "\"Mee-dau-mee-nau-bo,\" the messenger told him. It is a porridge of parched corn, cracked a little.", "The fox's little sharp nose went up. \"No, I thank you,\" he said, \"I can get plenty of that at home.\"", "Back went the messenger to tell the chief how contemptuously the fox had refused him.", "\"Go back to him,\" said the chief, \"and tell him we are going to have a nice fresh body, and we will have it cooked in the most delicate manner possible.\"", "That was a prospect the fox liked very much, and he accepted the second invitation with a will.", "When the hour came he set out for the chief's lodge. The company were ready for him; they had made common cause with the friend he had insulted. As he came in, the guest nearest the door rose with great courtesy and pressed his own seat on the new-comer. The next man was up immediately after and would hear of nothing but the fox taking his place, which stood nearer the fire and was therefore the post of honor. Then the third urged the same exchange on him with every expression of civility, and so, one ceremonious flourish after another, he was handed along the circle and always closer to the fire, where a huge cauldron stood with the good cheer still cooking in it. Occupying the highest place in the assembly suited the fox exactly. He also wanted a look into that kettle, a suspicion having grown on him that the delicacies he had come for might not be there at all.", "So by degrees they ushered him nearer and nearer the great blazing fire, and then one dexterous push and shove hoisted him up and into the seething kettle.", "It scalded his feet dreadfully. He got himself out and went home to his own lodge at a run, howling the whole way with the pain of it. His grandmother, who lived with him, as is the custom among the animals, demanded an account of the affair. He told her every circumstance faithfully — unlike the civilized animals, it never once occurred to him to tell his grandmother a story — and she reproved him very strongly.", "\"You have committed two great faults,\" she said. \"Originally, you were very rude to the chief who was so kind as to invite you, and by returning insult for civility you made yourself enemies who were determined to punish you. In the next place, it was very unbecoming in you to be so forward to take the place of honor. Had you been contented modestly to keep your seat near the door, you would have escaped the misfortune that has befallen you.\"", "None of which was much consolation to the poor fox, who went on whining and crying most piteously while his grandmother, having finished her lecture, set about binding up his wounds. A little dancing is held to add great virtue to any medical prescription or application, so, the dressing once in place, she began to dance — hard, and round and round the lodge.", "She had nearly danced herself out when the fox said, \"Grandmother, take off the bandages and see if my legs are healed.\"", "She took them off. The burns were as raw as before. So she danced, and danced again, stopping when his impatience got the better of him to lift the coverings and observe what the remedies had done. Toward morning she looked once more and found the burns healed clean away. \"But, oh!\" cried she, \"your legs are as black as a coal! They were so badly burned that they will never return to their color!\"", "Like many another brave, the fox was vain about his legs, and he broke into a transport of lamentation.", "\"Oh! my legs! My pretty red legs! What shall I do? The young girls will all despise me. I shall never dare to show myself among them again!\"", "He was sobbing still when his grandmother, worn out with her exercise, dropped off to sleep. By then he had settled his plan of revenge.", "He got up and stole out of the lodge and went quickly toward the chief's village. There he faced the principal lodge and barked. The people inside heard it come through the stillness of the night, and their hearts trembled in them. They knew what it meant: sorrow and trouble on its way to some one of their number.", "It was only a few days before the chief's beautiful daughter took sick. She grew rapidly worse, and neither medicines nor charms nor dances turned it, and in the end she died. This was not the shape in which the fox had wanted his misfortune to fall on the village, for he loved the chief's beautiful daughter, and he kept to his lodge and mourned and fretted over what he had done.", "A magnificent funeral was prepared. But her friends were in great perplexity. \"If we bury her in the earth,\" said they, \"the fox will come and disturb her remains. He has barked her to death, and he will be glad to come and finish his work of revenge.\"", "They talked it over among themselves and settled on hanging her body high up in a tree for its place of sepulture. The fox, they reckoned, would grope about in the ground and never think to raise his eyes into the branches over his head.", "The grandmother, though, had been at the funeral, and she came home and told the fox every part of it.", "\"Now, my son,\" she said, \"listen to me. Don't meddle with the remains of the chief's daughter. You have done mischief enough already. Leave her in peace.\"", "That night, as soon as his grandmother slept, the fox rambled forth. He had no trouble finding the place, and he settled himself under the tree they had put the young girl in. All the livelong night he gazed at her, and she was as beautiful as she had been in life. Then day broke, and with light enough to see her properly he found decay at its work: nothing beautiful left to her, only something loathsome.", "He went home sad and afflicted and spent the whole of the day in his lodge, mourning.", "\"Have you disturbed the remains of the chief's beautiful daughter?\" his parent asked him anxiously.", "\"No, grandmother,\" - and nothing more than that would he say.", "So it ran on, night after night and day after day. He was careful always to leave his watch at the first dawn, knowing her friends would suspect him and would come betimes to see that nothing had been touched.", "And by degrees he perceived that the morning light showed her less hideous than it had, and that little by little she was taking back the appearance she had presented in life, until in process of time her beauty and her look of health had come back to her whole.", "One day he asked for his pipe. \"Grandmother, give me my pipe, that I may take a smoke.\"", "\"Ah!\" said his grandmother, \"you begin to be comforted. You have never smoked since the death of the chief's beautiful daughter. Have you heard some good news?\"", "\"Never you mind,\" he told her, \"bring the pipe.\"", "He sat and smoked a long while. Then he said, \"Grandmother, sweep your lodge and put it all in order, for this day you will receive a visit from your daughter-in-law.\"", "She did as she was told, sweeping the lodge out and arranging it with all the taste she had in her, and then the two of them sat down to wait.", "\"When you hear a sound at the door,\" said the fox, \"you must give the salutation, and say, Come in.\"", "They had been sitting a while when a faint rustle reached the grandmother. She turned toward the doorway. The mat that generally hung there for a curtain had been rolled up, and the door stood open, which surprised her.", "\"Peen-tee-geen n'dau-nis!\" she called out.", "What came in was hardly more than a shadow, and a very faint one. Its outline sharpened as it came. She looked and kept looking, and out of it the form and the features of the chief's beautiful daughter gathered themselves; but a long time went by before the girl seemed like a reality at all, or sat down in the lodge like a creature of flesh and blood.", "The two of them kept it strictly to themselves, for nothing on earth would have induced them to let the bride's father or her friends learn what had happened. All the same, word got about before long that the chief's beautiful daughter was alive again and was living in the lodge of the Red Fox. Nobody could say how it ever got out; the grandmother certainly never breathed a word of it.", "However it traveled, the news set the village in an uproar. \"This must never be,\" said they all. \"He barked her to death once, and who knows what he may do next time?\"", "Her father came down on the question at once. \"The Red Fox isn't worthy of my daughter,\" he said. \"I had promised her to the Hart, the finest and most elegant among the animals. Now that she has returned to life, I shall keep my word.\"", "So the whole body of them marched on the Red Fox's lodge. Bridegroom, bride and grandmother resisted with everything they had, and numbers beat them; and the Hart, who had conveniently kept himself outside, well clear of any danger, took the chief's beautiful daughter up on his back and coursed off through the forest for his own home. But at his own door he turned his head, and the place where he had expected a bride was empty. His burden had felt very light to him from the start, and he had thought nothing of it, supposing lightness natural in a spirit come back from the dead. It never entered his head that she had glided from her seat at the very outset and slipped away, unobserved, in the middle of the tumult, back to the husband she had chosen.", "One or two more attempts were made by her friends to repossess themselves of the young creature, and not one of them succeeded. Then they said, \"Let her stay where she is. It is true the Red Fox occasioned her death, but by his watchfulness and care he caressed her into life again, so she rightfully belongs to him.\" So the Red Fox kept his beautiful bride, and the two of them lived on a long while together in great peace and happiness."],
  p3s24: ["A young man called Shee-shee-banze — the Little Duck — was working his canoe along the shore of the lake.", "A pair of girls came down to the water. The elder of them saw him and said to her sister, \"Let us call to him to take us a sail.\"", "It has to be said that whenever two or more sisters turn up in one of these Indian stories, the elder is without fail the foolish one, the ridiculous one, the one you are meant to find disgusting, and the younger is the wise and beautiful one.", "The younger objected. \"Oh, no,\" she said, \"let us not do such a thing. What will he think of us?\"", "Her sister paid no attention to that and called out across the water, \"Ho! come and take us into your canoe.\" He did as he was told. He brought the canoe in to the shore and took the two of them aboard.", "\"Who are you?\" the elder asked.", "\"I am Way-gee-mar-kin,\" he answered, \"the great chief.\"", "Now, the true Way-gee-mar-kin had something of the fairy about him. When his followers were gathered round him and he had a mind to be generous, he would give one small cough, and out of his mouth would come silver brooches and ear-bobs and ornaments of every kind, and his people would go down on the ground after them, each one grabbing for more than his share, exactly as people do in places that consider themselves more civilized.", "So the elder sister said, \"If you are Way-gee-mar-kin, let us see you cough.\"", "As it happened, Shee-shee-banze owned a few bits of silver himself, won in scrambles of that very sort, and he kept them packed away in the sides of his mouth against an emergency. He hacked and jerked and produced two or three, and the girl snatched them up as fast as they came.", "They went on down the shore. After a while a fine, tall elk stepped out of the timber and came to the water to drink.", "\"What is that?\" said the one who did all the talking. The younger had not spoken since they came aboard, and did not speak now.", "\"It is my dog that I hunt with.\"", "\"Call him to us, that I may see him.\"", "Shee-shee-banze called. The elk turned and fled back into the trees.", "\"He doesn't seem to obey you, though.\"", "\"No, it is because you inspire him with disgust, and so he flies from you.\"", "Not long after, a bear came out at the water's edge.", "\"What is that?\"", "\"One of my servants.\"", "Again she asked him to call it. Again the call was ignored, and again he gave the same reason for it.", "The outing ended at last. There had been magic in it the whole time, because the girls had believed themselves to be sitting in a canoe and there was no canoe at all. They had only imagined the thing under them.", "Shee-shee-banze lived in his grandmother's lodge, and that was where he took his two guests.", "He left them standing outside and went in by himself.", "\"Grandmother,\" he said, \"I have brought you two young girls, who will be your daughters-in-law. Invite them into your lodge.\"", "The old woman called out, \"Ho! come in.\" In they came, and they were made welcome, and given the best of everything the lodge had in it.", "Meanwhile the real Way-gee-mar-kin was getting up a great feast. When he handed his messenger the invitations he told him, \"Be very particular to bid Shee-shee-banze to the feast, for, as he is the smallest and meanest person in the tribe, you must use double ceremony with him, or he will be apt to think himself slighted.\"", "Shee-shee-banze was at home with his new friends when the messenger came to the door.", "\"Ho! Shee-shee-banze,\" he called, \"you are invited to a great feast that Way-gee-mar-kin is to give tonight, to all his subjects.\"", "Shee-shee-banze behaved as though nothing at all had been said. He whistled a little and looked off in another direction. The messenger said the whole thing over again, got the same nothing for his trouble, and went away.", "The girls looked at each other. At last the elder found her voice.", "\"What does this mean?\" she said. \"Why does he call you Shee-shee-banze, and invite you to visit Way-gee-mar-kin?\"", "\"Oh,\" said Shee-shee-banze, \"it is one of my followers that always likes to be a little impudent. I am obliged to put up with it sometimes, but you noticed that I treated him with silent contempt.\"", "The messenger went back and reported to the chief exactly how the invitation had been received.", "\"Oh,\" said the chief, who was a good-natured man, \"it is because he feels that he is poor and insignificant. Go back again - call him by my name, and make a flourishing speech to him.\"", "The messenger went and did precisely as he had been told.", "\"Way-gee-mar-kin,\" he announced, with every ounce of pomp he could raise, \"a great feast is to be given tonight, and I am sent most respectfully to solicit the honor of your company!\"", "\"Did I not tell you?\" said Shee-shee-banze to the two girls. Then he gave the small careless nod of a man used to such things and added, \"Tell them I'll come.\"", "When night came he painted himself with great care and put on his best feathers and every ornament he had. Before he left he drew his grandmother aside.", "\"Be sure,\" he said, \"that you watch these young people closely until I come back. Shut up your lodge tight, tight. Let no one come in or go out, and, above all things, don't go to sleep.\"", "Having given his orders, he went off.", "The grandmother fully meant to obey them. But the night wore on and her eyes kept falling shut, and at last she took a strong cord and laced it back and forth across the mat that hung over the doorway, the way the Indians lace up the mouth of a bag. The girls lay quiet in their bed. Everything was fast. She lay down herself and was soon sleeping very comfortably.", "The girls were not asleep at all. They were being eaten alive by curiosity about where Shee-shee-banze had gone, and the moment they were certain of the old woman they made ready to follow him and see for themselves. In case she woke and looked over, they took two logs of wood and laid them in the bed under the blanket, arranged so that anyone would have sworn two people were sleeping there quietly. Then they cut the cords at the door and went out, and the noise of the music and the dancing and the merry-making led them straight to the house of Way-gee-mar-kin.", "Inside, the chief sat on a throne in a blaze of light and splendor. The whole place was pleasure and amusement. Courtiers filled the room, every one of them turned out in the most brilliant array. The two of them searched the room for their friend Shee-shee-banze. He was not in it.", "Every so often the chief would cough, and silver and other precious things came showering out in every direction, and in the same instant the whole company was down on the floor scrambling to gather them up and keep them.", "It was in one of those rushes that the brides-elect finally caught sight of their poor little friend. He was crowded into a corner, where nobody took the smallest notice of him except to push him out of the way or step on him when he was underfoot. Each time one or another handled him so, he let out a pitiful little squeak. He was far too busy saving himself to notice that the pair he had shut up safe at home were standing there, watching every bit of it.", "At length the word came that the company should go. They all went, except the two young damsels. The chief had marked those two, and had already sent an assistant to them with great offers if they would stay and be his wives.", "Poor Shee-shee-banze came home to his lodge, and found to his consternation that the door stood open.", "\"Ho! grandmother,\" he cried, \"is this the way you keep watch?\"", "The old woman started up out of her sleep. \"There are my daughters-in-law,\" she said, and pointed to the two logs. He flung himself down on the ground between them, and struck them so hard that his back was broken, but he never minded that. His whole mind was on revenge and on getting his sweethearts back.", "He waited only long enough to fetch a powerful poison and make it ready. Then he stole softly back to Way-gee-mar-kin's wigwam. Nothing stirred anywhere. He got inside without making the least sound. The chief lay there, and a young girl slept against either side of him.", "All three of them were deep asleep. The chief lay face up, his mouth open wide. The poison was down his throat before he knew anything of it, and Shee-shee-banze went quietly home.", "In the morning it went round the village that Way-gee-mar-kin was dead — found so in his own bed. Everyone naturally supposed he had overindulged at his own feast. There was grief and lamentation everywhere. \"Let us go and tell poor Shee-shee-banze,\" said one of them, \"he was so fond of Way-gee-mar-kin.\"", "They found him sitting on a bank with a fishing line. He had been up since the peep of day, making his preparations for receiving the news.", "He had taken two or three fish already, cut the bladders out of them, run them full of blood and strapped them beneath his arm. The friends of the dead chief came in sight of him and shouted, \"Oh! Shee-shee-banze - your friend, Way-gee-mar-kin, is dead!\"", "He flung up his hands in despair, drew his knife, and drove it in — not into his heart at all, but into the blood-filled bladders he had made ready. He dropped to the ground, to all appearances a dead man, and the messengers at once began reproaching themselves. \"Oh! why did we tell him so suddenly? We might have known he wouldn't survive it. Poor Shee-shee-banze! he loved Way-gee-mar-kin so.\"", "The day after the funeral, to the great surprise of everybody, Shee-shee-banze came walking toward the dead chief's wigwam. As he came along he was singing — chaunting, rather, on a single flat note,[50] this: - \"Way-gee-mar-kin is dead, is dead, I know who killed him.", "I guess it was I - I guess it was I.\"", "The whole village came up at that. Every one of them went after the murderer, and he slipped through the lot of them and got away to a place where they could not touch him.", "Before long he showed himself again, walking with little mincing steps and singing to the same tune as before, - \"If you wish to take and punish me, Let the widows come and catch me.\"", "That struck everybody as a sound idea, and the young women were advised to go out and lure the culprit back into the village, where the dead chief's friends could lay hands on him.", "So out they went. He would let them get near him and then dance off a little way; would let them come nearer still, and then give ground again, singing the whole time, \"Come, pretty widows, come and catch me.\"", "That was how he drew them on, and he sweetened it with honeyed words and flattering speeches, until at last they agreed to go home with him and live in his lodge.", "It shocked the dead chief's people that the widows could turn so quickly, and they settled it among themselves that all three should be punished the moment they could be caught.", "They came round the lodge with shouting and threats. But he and his two brides had already slipped the watch and got down to his canoe, which lay in the river a few steps off.", "They were barely aboard when the escape was discovered. The whole troop came after them, and some went into the stream and got hold of the canoe, and in the struggle it went over. The instant they touched the water — whether the canoe's own magic did it, or the grandmother's necromancy, nobody can say — the three of them were turned into ducks and flew off quacking.", "Ever since that time, birds of that kind keep to parties of three — a male, and two females along with him.", "The Canard de France, or Mallard, and the Brancheuse, or Wood Duck, order themselves otherwise, and go through the air two by two. Of the Wood Duck they say his faithfulness runs so deep that once his mate is taken from him he will pair with no other bird at all, and grieves for her as long as he lives."],
  p3s25: ["With the payment finished and the Indians dispersed, we set about settling into our little home for a quiet season. The quiet did not last. A new source of disturbance came up.", "My husband, as Agent of the Winnebagoes, had sent his accounts of disbursements on to the Department at Washington, and the accounts had never arrived there. Word of that came back to him in due course — meaning by the roundabout, unreliable chain of hands that was all we had then for keeping any sort of contact with the Eastern world. The difficulty was plain enough. If the vouchers for what he had already spent could not be produced, and the recent payment of $15,000 in annuity money along with them, he would be standing in a very awkward position indeed. He decided to go straight to Washington and carry the duplicate accounts there himself.", "\"Should you like to go and see your father and mother,\" he said to me, one morning, \"and show them how the West agrees with you?\"", "A year had passed since I had seen them. Nothing he could have said would have pleased me more, and within a few days everything was ready for us to start.", "We had a guest with us just then — Miss Brush, of Detroit, who had come up from Green Bay in company with Mr. and Mrs. Whitney and Miss Frances Henshaw, the party being bound on an excursion to the Mississippi. Our little India-rubber house had stretched itself once again to hold all of them, and their visit was a very pleasant one.", "Two young ladies arriving at once was, as anyone can imagine, an outright godsend to the unmarried lieutenants. So when Miss Brush — weary of traveling, or else discouraged by the eight inches of snow that fell on the 4th of October — gave up the remainder of the excursion and accepted our urging that she stay with us until her friends came back through, we were regarded around the garrison as public benefactors. She would now come with us as far as Green Bay, and perhaps the whole way to Detroit.", "Nothing happened to us on the river going down. We came into Green Bay to find the entire place awake and restless, waiting on one of Mr. Newbery's schooners. From Fort Howard down to Dickenson's, there was no other subject. The vessel would bring friends to some and supplies to others, fashions for the ladies and news for the gentlemen, and it carried the mails besides — not merely for Green Bay, but for the whole of the upper country.", "She came in a few days later, and she had a mail aboard for Fort Winnebago. Only in the winter months did our letters travel overland to us, by way of Niles's Settlement and Chicago.", "It fell to my husband, as Postmaster, to open the bag, and he took out his own letters first. The first he read told him that the missing accounts had turned up at the Department and all was satisfactory there. The second was a sad disappointment. My parents had left for Kentucky for the winter — not for any city, not for anywhere a person could reach, but up the Sandy, and on over among the mountains of Virginia, chasing old land-claims that belonged to my grandfather's estate.", "There was no following them. In the short time we could be away from home we could hardly hope to come up with them, and that was true even allowing for the directions an old settler had once supplied to my father while he was exploring that wild country.", "\"You must go up Tug,\" said the man, \"and down Troublesome, and fall over on to Kingdom-come.\" An expedition of that description did not seem advisable to us. We turned around and made our way back to Fort Winnebago."],
  p3s26: ["There was not a boat ready to take us up the river. Our old friend Hamilton gave his word that one would be got into preparation immediately. The days went on. No boat appeared.", "November had begun by then. We were spending the time pleasantly enough — with the Irwins, with the Whitneys, and at the house of Colonel Stambaugh, the Indian Agent — but the delay was an inconvenience, and it had begun to vex me.", "So I proposed that we make the journey on horseback. The answer never varied. \"No, indeed,\" they told me. \"No mortal woman has ever gone that road, unless it was some native on foot, nor ever could.\"", "I put the other case to them. \"But suppose we set out in the boat and get frozen in on the way. We can neither pass the winter there, nor possibly find our way to a human home. We have had one similar experience already. Is it not better to take it for granted that I can do what you and others of your sex have done?\"", "The argument was overheard by Dr. Finley, post-surgeon at Fort Howard, who at once offered me the loan of Charlie, the horse he liked best. \"He is very sure-footed,\" the doctor alleged, \"and capital in a marsh or troublesome stream.\"", "That settled it: we would go by land. We sent for our old Menomonee friend \"Wish-tay-yun,\" who could guide as well over ground as over water, and the moment he came we started, leaving the trunks behind for Hamilton to forward whenever it might suit him to make good his intention about the boat.", "On the morning we left we held on till late for our fellow-travelers, Mr. Wing, of Monroe, and Dr. Philleo, of Galena. They did not come. Rather than waste more of the day we went without them, sure enough that the whole party would be together again at the Kakalin before the evening was out."],
  p3s26a: ["We crossed the river where Depere stands now and rode into the wild country on the west side, where nobody had settled anything. What we found was hill after wooded hill, and between each of them a ravine so narrow and so steep that I could not see how any creature except a mule or a goat would ever get through.", "Wish-tay-yun went first. His horse knew this country and had been schooled for roads of exactly this kind. Charlie, I have to say, was admirable. Whenever the ground dropped away in front of him he would plant his forefeet out ahead and go down on his haunches, sliding, entirely scientific about it, while I kept my seat by the only method available to me: a hard grip on the bridle and my whole body laid back and braced nearly flat against him. Then, with no warning, everything reversed. We would be climbing the far wall instead, and the danger now was going over backward into the ravine underneath us.", "There was comedy in it. Wish-tay-yun would scramble on up ahead and keep turning half around to see how I was faring behind him. And when I came over the top at last, out of breath and laughing, he would throw both hands into the air and shout, in tremendous glee, \"Mamma Manitou!\" (My mother is a spirit.)", "The Grignons, whom we had known a long while, could hardly believe I had attempted such a trip. They had never once attempted it themselves in all their years at the Kakalin — though there had never been any call for them to. If they wanted to see \"the Bay,\" they had only to take a canoe or a boat.", "Our gentlemen turned up at supper, exactly as we had expected. \"They had delayed to take dinner with Colonel Stambaugh - had had a delightful gallop up from: the Bay - had seen no ravines, nor anything but fine smooth roads - might have been asleep, but, if so, weren't conscious of it.\" That was their whole account of themselves, and it entertained us a good deal."],
  p3s26b: ["The next day's stage ran from the Kakalin to the Butte des Morts, where a man named Knaggs lived. It was rough, wild ground, not unlike what we had crossed the spring before between Hamilton's diggings and Kellogg's Grove, but this time we had Wish-tay-yun leading us instead of Uncle Billy, and that meant we could take it at a moderate pace.", "Forty miles was all it came to, and still I got down cold and worn out, so that Mrs. Knaggs's snug little room and the fire burning in it were a very welcome sight. We had put nothing in our mouths since morning except a few crackers carried loose in our pockets. I will admit that we fell on her coffee and her cakes without any ceremony whatever, and on the venison-steaks after them, and on the bear's meat, which I had never in my life tasted before.", "Supper finished, we began to consider where we were going to lie down. The room we had eaten in was small — a bed, a table shoved against the wall, and the handful of chairs we were sitting on, and that was the whole of it. There was not floor enough left for a shakedown of any description.", "\"Where can you put us for the night?\" asked my husband of Mr. Knaggs, when he made his appearance.", "\"Why, there is no place that I know of, unless you can camp down in the old building outside.\"", "We walked out to see it. One room, bare, filthy. A great chimney took up most of one side with a few brands still smoking in it. A bunk of sorts, rickety, had been built against another wall, and that was every stick of furniture the place contained. The floor lay thick with mud and dirt, and sitting on it near the fire was an old Indian with a pan of boiled corn in his lap, scooping the corn up in both hands and eating it as fast as he could get it down.", "It took us a moment to understand that he was blind. Hearing feet and voices come in at the door, he pulled the pan in tight against himself and started up a low, sour muttering. Then he was told that the man speaking to him was Shaw-nee-aw-kee, and everything in his face let go. He even put the dish out toward us and asked us to eat with him.", "\"But are we to stay here?\" I asked. \"Can we not sleep out-of-doors?\"", "\"We have no tent,\" replied my husband, \"and the weather is too cold to risk the exposure without one.\"", "\"I could sit in a chair all night, by the fire.\"", "\"Then you wouldn't be able to ride to Bellefontaine tomorrow.\"", "So that was decided. All the bedding Mr. Knaggs had to offer was one small bear-skin. The boards of the bunk were a shade less foul than the floor, so we laid the skin over them, and our saddle-blankets over the skin, and set a pair of saddle-bags at the head for a bolster. I rolled myself in my cloak and gave myself up to it.", "Lying flat after a day in the saddle rested me a little, I suppose. Sleep was out of the question. It would have been out of the question on a far softer bed, because the old Indian snored, and so did the two or three others who had joined him, and every so often he would find himself crowded in the dark and cry out about it in that same querulous voice.", "Getting up at first light and making ready to ride was a relief. I never learned where our fellow-travelers had stowed themselves, though it was plain they had done no better than we had. They were in high spirits regardless. We ate our breakfast in good humor and were ferried across the river, and picked up the trail on the far side toward Bellefontaine, twelve miles out from Fort Winnebago."],
  p3s26c: ["The great bug-bear of this road was the next thing in our way, and we came to it at about nine o'clock. Ma-zhee-gaw-gaw Swamp lay across our way, half a mile from edge to edge and running off to either side further than the eye could follow. Water stood over much of it. Out of the water rose black knobs, scattered anyhow, and those knobs were all the footing our animals were going to get. Where there was no water there was mud instead, black and oozy, the sort of surface that looks as though it will open under a hoof and let the leg go down as far as it likes.", "That was what we had to cross. All three of the gentlemen pushed out ahead of me, each one announcing that he meant to find the safest and firmest line for me to follow. One would call back, \"Here, madam, come this way!\" Another would call, \"This is the best path, wifie, follow me.\" Charlie, more often than not, was of a different opinion, and chose his own route, and his was the best route in the swamp.", "He took it slowly. He would stand on one little rise of ground and study the next, and step across, and stand again, and now and then he would refuse a stretch of mud altogether and go around it, and by this method the rest of the party gradually drew away from us. I had been watching nothing but my horse's feet. When at last I lifted my eyes, this is what I saw: my husband down off his horse, hauling at the bridle to get the animal out of the place it had walked itself into; Mr. Wing's great white beast lying over on its side in the mud, floundering, with Mr. Wing working to free himself from the stirrups; and Dr. Philleo standing a little apart from his own steed, which had pitched into a deep bog and was heaving to get up out of it. It was a formidable sight. All three of them found their voices at once. \"Oh, don't come this way!\"", "\"Indeed,\" cried I, \"I have no thought of it. Charlie and I know better.\" I left it entirely to the sagacious creature, and he went on picking and choosing until he had carried me safely past the whole dismounted company. I looked back at them climbing toward their saddles again, and I am afraid I could not keep myself from a small triumphant flourish of the whip.", "Three hours we spent in that place, and still we were not out of the Slough of Despond. At last the far edge came in sight — and with it a stream, deep, and five or six feet across. The gentlemen reached it first, got down, and stood there debating.", "\"Jump off, jump off, madam,\" cried Mr. Wing, and \"Jump off, jump off,\" echoed Dr. Philleo, \"we are just consulting how we are to get you across.\"", "\"What do you think about it?\" asked my husband.", "\"Charlie will show you,\" replied I. \"Come, Charlie.\" I lifted the bridle smartly, gave his neck a pat and made the little chirping sound he knew, and he went over that water as lightly as a deer and set me down on solid ground.", "Of the whole company Mr. Wing had come off worst. The clumsy animal he was riding held the view that whenever he found himself in difficulty his part was to lie down and submit to whatever came next, and his rider, who was neither light nor especially quick, was generally beneath him and half bedded in the mire before he had settled on what to do about it.", "So it was good sense in him, when he came to the little stream, to walk straight into it and wade across, and rinse off what he could of the morning from his horse and from himself. The other gentlemen had no better idea, and did the same."],
  p3s26d: ["On the dry ground past the morass we gave ourselves only a few minutes. Bellefontaine lay forty-five miles ahead of us, and not one of those miles was of the short, obliging kind.", "The horses were magnificent all through the afternoon. Charlie never broke his canter. But the light was going out of the sky, and none of the landmarks that were supposed to tell us we were close had shown themselves.", "\"Can we not stop and rest for a few moments under one of the trees?\" I asked. I was very nearly finished, and a cold November rain had been coming down on us for hours.", "\"If it were possible, we would,\" came the answer, \"but see how dark it is growing. If we should lose our way, it would be worse than being wet and tired.\"", "So we rode on. Right at dusk we crossed a clear little stream. \"That,\" my husband said, \"is, I think, two miles from Bellefontaine. Cheer up - we shall soon be there.\" That put heart into all of us, and we went forward in better spirits. We covered one mile, and another, and another, and no light showed anywhere among the trees.", "\"We have certainly traveled more than six miles now,\" I said.", "\"Yes - that couldn't have been the two-mile creek.\"", "It was eight o'clock when Bellefontaine finally took us in. They brought us into a big room with a huge fire blazing in it. Mr. Wing and Dr. Philleo had got there ahead of us, and there were other travelers besides, come up from the Mississippi. The hostess was an enormous woman, known to everyone as \"la grosse Américaine,\" and she received me with a torrent of kindness and talk, put me in the arm-chair in the warmest corner, and in her own good time laid an excellent supper in front of us.", "Her generosity stopped short of her one bed. What she did instead was to heap everything soft she owned onto the bare floor in front of the fire and settle me on it as best she could. Then, watching my husband fuss over me and worry that the rain and the long ride would make me ill, she said, in a voice full of admiring sympathy, \"How kind your companion is to you!\" We had never heard the word used that way before, and it entertained us a great deal.", "Our fellow travelers left early next morning for the Fort, only twelve miles off, and they were good enough to carry a note home for us, asking that Plante be sent out with the carriage to bring us in over the last of the road.", "We came into the Portage safe and whole, and that was the end of the first overland journey any white woman had ever made from Green Bay to Fort Winnebago. My own opinion of myself rose considerably when my husband told me what I had covered the day before, from Knaggs's to Bellefontaine — sixty-two miles."],
  p3s27: ["A few weeks after we got home, my husband took his mother down to Prairie du Chien to be seen by Dr. Beaumont, of the U.S. Army, for the benefit of his medical advice.", "They went in a large open boat down the Wisconsin, and since the boat had to make the trip anyway it was decided to bring back a good supply of corn, enough to carry the men and the cattle through the winter.", "The ice caught them. It formed in the river so early that he had barely started up with his load before he had to turn and take the whole of it back to the Prairie, and sit there, and wait — wait until the winter's ice lay thick enough to hold sleighs, and then make the journey a second time and bring the corn up over the top of the river he could not travel on. That is what it cost, in time and labor and exposure, to move the plain necessities of life from one point to another in that wild and desolate country.", "My brother Arthur came up from Kentucky by way of the Mississippi in the latter part of April, and he brought the news with him. There was new trouble with the Sauks and Foxes. Black Hawk had recrossed the Mississippi with the flower of his nation, to take back the old homes and the old corn-fields. Our own Indians began coming in almost at once to confirm it and to tell us, over and over, that they meant to stay faithful friends to the Americans. Then came word that the Illinois Rangers were in the Rock River country, and that the regular force under General Atkinson was in pursuit of the hostile Indians — who, by every report that reached us, were never where the pursuit was. It is not their custom to stand and give battle. The Sauks simply scattered themselves through the country and waited on a lucky accident to let them fall upon their enemies unexpectedly. Such accidents came, alas, only too often.", "So the army's experience, for the most part, was this: a long, toilsome, punishing march to the place where the Sauks were certainly waiting to receive them, and then the discovery that the rogues had scampered off to some entirely different part of the country.", "Wherever they did go, they left the most atrocious barbarities behind them, though the worst of those had not reached our ears yet. All we were told, at the time, was that they were down in the neighborhood of the Rock River and the Kishwaukee, and that they let no opportunity pass of falling upon defenceless inhabitants and murdering them cruelly.", "Once it was clear the Sauks and Foxes would not do what they had done the year before — retreat peaceably across the Mississippi — Mr. Kinzie resolved to hold a council with every principal chief of the Winnebagoes he could reach. He knew the Sauks would spend every effort trying to bring their neighbors in with them, and he knew, too, that in the breasts of too many of the young men there was a real hunger to distinguish themselves by taking some white scalps. They did not love the Americans. Why should they? By the Americans they had been dispossessed, piece by piece, of the broad and beautiful country of their forefathers, and hunted from place to place, and all they had received in exchange was a few thousands a year in silver and presents, together with the pernicious example, the debasing influence, and the outright ill treatment of far too many of the new settlers on their land."],
  p3s28: ["With all of that in front of him, their Father judged that nothing short of the utmost watchfulness would do, and that the strongest arguments he had would have to be spent keeping the young men of the Winnebagoes in their allegiance to the Americans. The older men he was sure of. About fifty lodges had come in when the trouble first started and made their camp around our house, and what they told us was that if the Sauks meant to get at us they would have to kill them first. We knew those men. We believed them absolutely.", "But having them close, while it made us feel guarded, also opened a channel for the most exciting and agitating communications, and they arrived daily. As the fighting worked nearer and nearer, their runners carried the word in. That Captain Barney's head had been recognized in the Sauk camp, where it had been carried the day before. Then that the Sauks were marching to meet the whites with Lieutenant Beall's head carried on a pole ahead of them. Sometimes a story turned out, unhappily, to be exactly true, as with the murder of their own Agent, M. St Vrain, at Kellogg's Grove — killed by the Sauks themselves, who of all people ought to have protected him."],
  p3s29: ["It was after that last piece of news that the appointed council with the Winnebagoes came due, to be held at the Four Lakes, thirty-five miles from Fort Winnebago.", "We pleaded with him. We argued. None of it moved him an inch. \"It was his duty to assemble his people and talk to them,\" my husband said, \"and he must run the risk, if there were any. He had perfect confidence in the Winnebagoes. The enemy, by all he could learn, were now far distant from the Four Lakes — probably at Kosh-ko-nong. He would set off early in the morning with Paquette, hold his council, and return to us the same evening.\"", "I will not try to describe that day. It was long and dreary and there was nothing in it to do but wait through it. By nightfall we had come to the state where a drunken Indian calling out, or even a dog barking, was enough to fill us with terror.", "We sat up late at the open window, listening to everything. And at last, out of all that listening, came the sound of horses. We knew the tread of Griffin and Jerry coming up the hill, and then a cheerful shout came up ahead of them, which meant that all was well. He and his interpreter had ridden seventy miles that day, and held a long talk with the Indians in the middle of it.", "The Winnebagoes in council had promised to do everything in their power to keep peace and good order among their young men. They told their Father that the bands on the Rock River, Win-no-sheek's excepted, were all determined to stay friendly and keep clear of the Sauks — and that to prove it they were giving up their villages and their corn-fields and moving north, so that their Great Father the President should have no cause to be dissatisfied with them. About Win-no-sheek and his people they said they could not answer.", "Time went on and brought more of the same. The murders of Auberry, Green, and Force at Blue Mound. The attack on Apple Fort. That last came to us from old Crély, the father of Mrs. Paquette, who rode express from Galena, and who swore he had once passed a bush with Sauks hiding behind it — but his horse caught the smell of the sweet-scented grass they always wear on a war-party, and set off at a gallop that did not stop until it reached the Portage.", "Another man who brought us news was a young gentleman named Follett. He had spent so many days watching for the enemy that his eyes had come out and set, and it was a long while after he reached safety before they went back into their usual place and expression."],
  p3s29a: ["Among the rumors that came to us in those days was one that the Sauks were considering an attack on Fort Winnebago itself. That the fort was in no state to be attacked, the Indians knew perfectly well. Every effective man had been pulled out of it on a requisition from General Atkinson and sent to join him at his newly built fort at Kosh-ko-nong.", "Fort Winnebago was not picketed in. The barracks and the officers' quarters had no defences at all beyond thin panelled doors and Venetian blinds — nothing that would hold out long against clubs or hatchets. There was no artillery. The Commissary's store was outside the bounds of the fort altogether, down under the hill.", "Mr. Kinzie had been pointing all of this out to the officers from the beginning. What he generally got back was a laugh.", "\"Never fear,\" they would say. \"The Sauks aren't coming here to attack us.\"", "One afternoon we were over in the garrison visiting some of the ladies, with several officers in the room, and the talk turned, as it always did now, to how matters stood.", "\"Do you not think it wiser,\" I asked a blustering young officer, \"to be prepared against possible danger?\"", "\"Not against these fellows,\" he said, with contempt.", "\"I don't think I would even take the trouble to fasten the blinds to my quarters.\"", "\"At least,\" I said, \"if you some night find a tomahawk raised to cleave your skull, you will have the consolation of remembering that you haven't been one of those foolish fellows who keep on the safe side.\"", "That nettled him a little, and he was more nettled still when sister Margaret said: \"Personally, I am of Governor Cass's opinion. He was at Chicago during the Winnebago war. We were all preparing to move into the fort on the first alarm. Some were for being brave and delaying, like our friends here. 'Come, come,' said the Governor, 'hurry into the fort as fast as possible — there is no merit in being brave with the Indians. It is the height of folly to stay and meet danger which you may by prudence avoid.'\""],
  p3s29b: ["Within a few days our friends woke up to the fact that something had to be done, and done at once. The first step was an order forbidding any Winnebago to come inside the garrison, in case they should find out what they had known just as well as we had for the past three months — how little there was in that place to resist anything. The next was to send fatigue-parties out into the woods under guard, to cut pickets to fence the garrison in.", "There was every reason to think the enemy were not far off. The best guess about their purpose in coming north was that they meant to break a road through into the Chippewa country, where they would be safe among friends and allies. But the story our Indian runners brought in most often was a different one. It was that the Sauks had determined to come down on the whites at the Portage and the Fort and massacre every one of them, sparing only the families of the Agent and the Interpreter.", "Plante and Pillon had taken their families out at the first word of danger. What was left to us was Manaigre, whose wife was half Winnebago, Isidore Morrin, and the two blacksmiths from Sugar Creek, Mâtâ and Turcotte.", "At night we were armed, all of us, and each had a post. Every possible thing was done to make the house secure, and then the orders were given. If we were attacked, sister Margaret and I were to take the children up to the rooms above while my husband and his men held the house as long as they could hold it. I had lately shown my sportsmanship by bringing down a blackbird on the wing, entirely by accident, and on the strength of that I believed I could do some real execution with my little pistols. They were laid beside my pillow every night. I was fully resolved to use them. What I remember, and what I find I cannot explain away, is that the idea of taking the lives of two Sauks — which is exactly what I should have done — did not trouble me in the slightest. It explained to me something I had often wondered at: how a soldier on a field of battle can be indifferent to the destruction of human life. Had I ever actually been called on to use those weapons to any effect, I have no doubt at all that I would have looked back on it with horror.", "We were ringed by Indian lodges, and a camp is never quite silent, and our nerves were in the state that a steady diet of such news puts them in. We slept badly. One night, long after we had settled ourselves as well as we could, there came a tap on the window at the head of our bed, and a voice calling: \"Chon! Chon!\" — John! John!"],
  p3s29c: ["\"Tshah-ko-zhah?\" — What is it?", "It was Hoo-wau-ne-kah, the Little Elk. He spoke fast, and there was great agitation in it. I could not follow him. I lay there shaking, waiting for somebody to turn it into English for me, and dreading it. Every so often a word came through that I did know. Sau-kee. Shoonk-hat-tay-rah, horse. Neither of them was any comfort.", "The trouble, as I learned soon enough, was this. A fresh trail had been found near the Petit Rocher, on the Wisconsin, and the people at the villages on the Barribault were badly frightened, afraid it might be the Sauks. By the look of it something like a hundred horses or more had gone by that way. Hoo-wau-ne-kah had been sent off at once to tell their Father and ask what he made of it.", "His Father heard him out and then told him the trail was almost certainly General Henry's troops, who were said to have come north hunting for the enemy. The marks of the hoofs, by his own account of them, showed the horses had been shod. That settled it. No Sauk trail would look like that. The people at the villages had nothing to be uneasy about.", "\"Very well, Father,\" said Hoo-wau-ne-kah. \"I will go back and tell my people what you say. They will believe you, for you always tell them the truth. You aren't like us Indians, who sometimes deceive each other.\" And he went back to his friends much easier in his mind than he had come.", "The picketing and the other defences were finished about then, and a detachment of troops came up from Fort Howard under Lieutenant Hunter, which between them made the fort the safest place on the Portage. So every evening, just before dusk, we formed up and marched across to the other side of the river, to the quarters that had been given us inside the garrison, and left our own house and everything in it to the care of the Frenchmen and our friends the Winnebagoes."],
  p3s29d: ["It was on one of those days that we were sitting at the windows that look out over the Portage — we were seldom anywhere else by then, our whole occupation being to watch the road and see what was coming next — when a whoop reached us from a long way off, loud and shrill and drawn out. The news-halloo. Something had happened. We stared down the road to its farthest end and made out a body of horsemen moving toward us, and as they came on we saw they were Indians. They were in full costume. Scarlet streamers ran from the ends of their lances, and their arms threw back the sun. Nearer, and the paint came up, and the feathers, and the brooches. Fifty warriors, or more than fifty. They came to the turning that goes down to the Fort and passed it, and rode straight on up the hill to the Agency. Shaw-nee-aw-kee was not at the house. The Interpreter had been sent for the moment we first saw them at a distance, and had not yet come. The party rode to the top, halted near the blacksmith's shop, and did not get down.", "Our hearts went over. It could only be the enemy. And at that moment my husband came into view, walking up from the direction of the Interpreter's house. We called out to him to stop. He went on toward them.", "Then the chief of the party swung down off his horse, and every man behind him did the same, and they came forward to shake hands, and our joy at the sight of it was past describing.", "They cleared a space around the leader and my husband, and the leader began to speak. He flourished a sword as he spoke and threw his arms about violently. It was the first time I had ever seen an Indian carrying that weapon, and I hated the sight of it in such hands. There were moments when he looked as though he were about to take my husband's head off at a stroke, and we sat there remembering the stratagems at Mackinac and Detroit in the old days. At last the speech ended. Hands were shaken again. Then we saw my husband turn and lead the way to his storehouse, and shortly his men carried out tobacco and pipes and laid them at the chief's feet.", "Our suspense was ended by being told who these people were. It was Man-Eater, the principal chief of the Rock River Indians, come in with his band to hold a talk and bring us word.", "Those Indians were under the special care of Mr. Henry Gratiot, whose efforts to keep this — the most dangerous portion of the Winnebagoes — in good feeling had been unremitting, and very shrewdly judged.", "What Man-Eater had come to tell us, and he was as noble an Indian in character as he was in appearance, only confirmed what we already had. The Sauks were working north, toward the Portage. He plainly did not know where they were."],
  p3s29e: ["Not long after Man-Eater's people had taken their leave, another party of Winnebagoes arrived, and these asked leave to dance for their Father.", "The compliment was accepted. They gathered, as they always did, on the esplanade in front of the house. My sister, the children, and I took our usual places at the open windows, and my husband sat down on the broad step at the door, the door that opens straight from the outside air into the parlor where we were.", "The dance began. They went round and round, each man following the back of the man ahead of him, and as they came past my sister, Mrs. Helm, said to me, \"Look at that small, dark Indian, with the green boughs on his person — that is a Sauk! They always mark themselves in this manner with white clay, and ornament themselves with leaves when they dance!\" I had never seen that costume on any of our own people. I looked at him properly then: green chaplets bound round his head and round his legs, and his gun wreathed the same way, and on his body no paint at all except the white transverse streaks laid over the whole of it. He was a stranger. I want to be exact, because fear will improve a face. It did not need to improve his. His face was ferocious.", "He carried the gun in his hand, and every circuit of the dance brought him directly in front of where we sat, and every time he came he turned his eyes full on us and clubbed the weapon in front of him, and we read defiance in it. We sat still as death. To show any sign of fear would have been the worst thing we could do, and we both knew it. Then my sister said, quite low, \"I have always thought that I was to lose my life by the hands of the Indians. This is the third Indian war I have gone through, and now, I think, it will be the last.\"", "It was the only time in my life I ever saw her lose her self-possession. She was the calmest and most resolute woman I have known, and I could see now that she was trembling. And still we sat there. There was a kind of fascination in it — the more frightened we became, the less able we were to look away. Then a few drops of rain began to fall. The Indians danced on for a minute or two more, and then, all at once, with whooping and shouting, they rushed the house. We fled into my room and shut the door. My sister held it fast at first, then let go of it and came and sat beside me on the bed, because she saw that I could not get hold of myself. Of all the ways there are to die, death at the hands of savages is the hardest one to face calmly, and I believed with my whole mind that our hour had come.", "The dance never stopped. They simply carried it into the parlor and went on with it there, leaping and yelling until it seemed the roof would come down on top of us. Through a crack in the door we begged my husband and the children to come in with us. The children would not — they were having far too fine a time, and it had not entered their heads that anything was wrong. My husband came for a moment to reassure me and then judged it wisest to go back out, and stand there, and show by his presence that he was pleased by the compliment. He made light of our fears. He would not allow that the object of our suspicions was any Sauk at all, but only some young Winnebago got up in their fashion, which is a thing they sometimes do.", "It may well have been good fun to him, to go home to his village and tell how he frightened the white squaws. A white boy would do the same trick without thinking twice about it. Human nature being everywhere much the same, I do not see why it should be so unlikely in an Indian."],
  p3s30: ["The danger had come close enough by then that my husband decided to send his family to Fort Howard, which everyone believed lay well outside the enemy's range. I pleaded to be allowed to stay. It made no difference at all. He was firm.", "\"I must not leave my post,\" he said, \"while there is any danger. My departure would maybe be the signal for an immediate alliance of the Winnebagoes with the Sauks. I am certain that as long as I am here my presence will act as a restraint upon them. You wish to stay and share my dangers! Your doing so would expose us both to certain destruction in case of attack. By the aid of my friends in both tribes, I could hope to preserve my own life if I were alone, but surrounded by my family, that would be impossible — we should all fall victims together. My duty plainly is, to send you to a place of safety.\"", "The means of doing it turned up soon enough. Paquette, the Interpreter, was also an agent of the American Fur Company, and he had a boat-load of furs to send down to Green Bay, on their way to Mackinac. Mr. Kinzie had the boat made as comfortable as an open boat of that kind can be made — a tent-cloth stretched over a framework of hoop-poles across the center and lined with a dark-green blanket — put an abundant store of provisions and other comforts aboard, and gave us into the joint care of my brother Arthur and our faithful blacksmith, Mâtâ.", "Mâtâ was a tall, gaunt Frenchman with a freckled face and a great crop of crisp, sandy hair, and an incurable determination to speak English. His command of the language was not large, and he made it stranger still by putting an s on the end of nearly every word and delivering each phrase with a jerk.", "\"Davids,\" he would say to the little yellow fiddler, after an evening's frolic at the Interpreter's, \"Davids, clear away the tables and the glasses, and play fishes-hornspikes.\" He was a kind, affectionate creature, and his devotion to Monsieur Johns and Madame Johns had no limit that anyone ever found.", "Besides those two, three trusty Indians were engaged to escort us, the chief of them a man called Old Smoker. The crew of the boat were all French engagés in the service of the Fur Company — six of them, gay-hearted and merry, easing the work with their pipes and their songs, and they would have taken it as a high compliment if the ladies aboard had joined in the singing. Our hearts were too heavy that day to give them the pleasure.", "We left home on the Fourth of July, and it was a gloomy day for those of us who went and for the one who stayed. None of us knew whether we would ever see one another again. Several of that circle had been through Indian warfare before and knew exactly what the worst looked like. And there was not even the comfort of knowing that going was safe. The Sauks might be on the other side of us. The road we were taking might — not likely, but it might — carry us straight into the middle of them. So it was a solemn leave-taking, and every one of us felt it might be a parting for this world.", "Not every one. The Frenchmen laughed and sang and cracked their jokes and assured Monsieur John that they would take Madame John and Madame Alum safe to the bay, spite of Sauks or wind or weather.", "So we set off. The Fort stayed in sight a long while, the river swinging us in toward its walls and out away from them and in again, and it was nearly mid-day before we lost the last of it and our home with it.", "At the noon-tide meal — the voyageurs measure their day in pipes — somebody made an alarming discovery. No bread had been put aboard for the crew. How it had happened, nobody could say. One man was positive a large quantity had been brought over from the garrison bakery that very morning for their use; another had seen the sacks of loaves standing in Paquette's kitchen with his own eyes. Wherever the bread was, it was not with us, and we were many miles down the river with nothing to feed six Frenchmen but some salted pork, a few beans, and some onions. We held a consultation. Should the boat turn back to the Portage for supplies? But the same danger that had put us on the water in the first place was still there, and we had been ordered to make all possible speed. On examination our own store of bread and crackers turned out to be far more than we could ever want, and we judged it would stretch to rations for the Frenchmen as far as Powell's, at the Butte des Morts, which we would reach the day after the next. We would certainly get a fresh supply there.", "That settled, we went on — and from then on we went in total silence. A song or a loud laugh was flatly forbidden until we should be past the outermost limit of the country where the enemy might be. We had been told before we started about a certain point on the river, where the low marshy meadows it had been winding through rise into firmer, higher ground. That is the border of the Menomonee territory. It is also the place where the Sauks, if they had crossed north of the Wisconsin and were making for the Chippewa country, would most likely be met."],
  p3s31: ["On the forenoon of the second day word reached us that we were coming up on that place. I have to own it: we held our breath for awe.", "The three Winnebagoes were up in the bow. Old Smoker, the chief, sat squatted on his feet on the bench of the foremost rowers. We looked at him. He was staring hard at the wooded point ahead of us, and we followed the line of his eyes, and while we were looking three Indians stepped forward and stood on the bank. We said it to each other under our breath: if they are Sauks, we are lost, because the whole body of them will be in that thicket. The boat went on. Nobody spoke. There was the dip of the paddle, and there was, I think, the sound of some hearts, and nothing else at all. We looked back at the chief. His nostrils were flared open and his eyes were almost glaring.", "Then he came off that bench in a single bound and let out his long, shrill whoop.", "\"Hoh! hoh! hoh! Neechee — friend — Muh-no-mo-nee!\"", "After that it was all gladness. Every one of us was pressing forward to shake hands with the strangers the moment we could reach them, purely out of joy that they were Menomonees and not the others. They could tell us nothing whatever about the Sauks.", "By noon that day we counted ourselves clear of the dangerous country. We were still careful. When the boat was pushed ashore for the mid-day pipe, under a fine overhanging bank with thick woods on top of it, the watchfulness slackened a little, and the young people were let out to wander a short way from the boat with Arthur and Mâtâ minding them.", "They were back before long. The woods, they said, were alive with pigeons — you could nearly knock them down with a stick — and would we please, please let them shoot just enough for supper. No. The enemy might be closer than we thought, and one shot fired would tell them exactly where we were, and the wise thing was to give notice to nobody, friend or foe. They went back to the boat without their game, and not happily.", "The next morning brought us to Powell's, at the Butte des Morts. There was not a loaf of bread in the place, and the faces of the poor Frenchmen went sad at the news of it. Our own store was finished by then too. All that could be got as a substitute was a bag of dark, bitter-looking flour. That was the provision for our whole party, and we had to be content with it, and we left the Hillock of the Dead feeling that it had been the grave of our hopes as well."],
  p3s31a: ["Hard rowing brought us before long to the place where the river runs out into that beautiful sheet of water, Winnebago Lake. There was hardly any wind when we came onto it, but the Frenchmen hoisted their sail anyway, in hope of being spared the labor of rowing across. Then they whistled for a wind. They whistled with the whole force of their lungs and nothing came of it. Then they appealed to La Vierge, in that comical mixture of joking and real reverence they keep for such occasions, and nothing came of that either. At last somebody proposed the only remaining course: the goddess of the winds must be propitiated with an offering of some cast-off garment.", "Guardapié, who spoke for the crew in all matters, made the application all round. Not one of the poor voyageurs owned a spare article of any kind. A few old rags were finally dug out of the little hold in the bow where they keep their food and their clothing and their dirt in one heap, and these were thrown into the waves. For a moment everybody believed it had worked. The sail stirred, filled a little — and then flapped down idly against the mast. The party were in despair. Then Julian and Edwin put their heads together, whispered, and stepped forward as messengers of mercy. In a trice they had their jackets and vests off and were offering the crew the next garment in, for the raising of the wind.", "The boatmen hesitated. It seemed to them too magnificent a gift to accept. But a preparatory shake or two revealed that the contribution was worth rather less than they had supposed, and with a great shout of laughter they gave it to the waves. To the enormous delight and astonishment of the boys, a breeze got up at that exact moment and carried the little vessel beautifully along, about half of the way to Garlic Island. There the charm ran out. It could not be renewed. Further offerings of the same kind produced nothing at all, and every other expedient failed too, and the Frenchmen went back to their oars with some distinctly disrespectful remarks about the lady whose help they had asked for.", "Two or three canoes of Winnebagoes came in at the island at the same time we did, and their owners came straight up with an offering of sturgeon they had taken in the lake. As this promised a pleasant change for the noon meal — for the Frenchmen at least — we decided to stop and kindle a fire and cook it. We used the interval to send the boys off to the far side of the island, where the water is clear and shallow over a pebbly beach and altogether too tempting to pass up. Harry, the black boy, went along to watch them bathe, and we amused ourselves picking the fine red raspberries that grow all over that island.", "Our enjoyment was cut short by a discovery. The whole place — the vines, the shrubs, and by every appearance the ground itself — was crawling with wood-ticks. The wood-tick fastens on the skin and then works its way into the flesh, and what it leaves behind is a swelling and an irritation that is exceedingly painful and can be dangerous. We raised the alarm and got the boys back at speed to the open, well-trodden part of the island. But we had not left our tormentors behind us. We were conscious of them all that day. They were not done with us for several days after, and the ones that had managed to bury themselves had to be taken out with the point of a penknife or a large needle. When dinner was over we boarded the boat, and the wind having risen in earnest at last, it carried us down to the far verge of the lake and into the mouth of the river beyond — the Winnebago Rapids, they call that stretch.", "On the point of land to the right stood a cluster of neat bark wigwams. That was Four-Legs' village."],
  p3s31b: ["The run down the rapids was a lively business and not entirely a safe one, and at the end of it came the Grand Chûte, a fall of several feet, which the boat went over and came out of right side up. Toward evening we reached the settlement of the Waubanakees at the head of the Little Chûte. These are the Stockbridge, or Brothertown, Indians — what is left of the old Mohicans — who had come out a few years before from Oneida County, in the State of New York, to a tract the United States granted them on the fertile banks of the Fox River. They had already cut wide openings into the forest and put up substantial, comfortable houses near the water, where the banks stand high and carry, for most of their length, enormous trees.", "We decided to ask these people for hospitality, though only a corner of somebody's fire, enough to boil our tea-kettle and bake the short-cake that had stood in for bread for nearly two days now. The making of that cake had been the great joke of the trip. Powell's black flour, some salt, and a little butter went into the tin box that had held our meat; the box was then turned over, and scoured, and became the dough-board; the vinegar-bottle was the rolling-pin; a shallow tin dish did for the baking. The Waubanakees were good enough to lend us an iron bake-kettle as well, and to stand over the cake and see it properly cooked, once Harry had carried it up to their house.", "They were so kind about all of it that the crew took the notion of asking for a lodging on shore — three nights packed into that boat had been enough for them. Arthur and Mâtâ came round to the same idea shortly after, and we were pressed to do likewise, with the assurance that the houses were extremely neat and orderly.", "We chose to stay aboard. It was a fine night and everything in the boat had been arranged so comfortably, and we kept Edwin and Josette with us.", "The boat was moored tight, because the Little Chûte lay just below us, and a craft that broke loose in that current and went down over the falls would be a very serious matter indeed. As a further precaution one man was left aboard to keep everything safe and in order. Then the rest of them climbed the bank and went off to their beds in the Waubanakee cabins.", "It was a beautiful, calm, moonlit night, the air just warm enough to be pleasant, and the low steady murmur of the rapids and the fall not far off put our party to sleep in no time. How long we slept I could not say. What woke us was a rush of wind. It caught the poles holding up the awning and bent them, and then snapped them, and a second gust took the tent and the blanket clean off the boat and away downstream. The moonlight was gone. Then a flash of lightning showed them to us, once, far off down the water, sailing away like a spectre in the distance."],
  p3s31c: ["The storm got worse. The rain came down in torrents, and the thunder and the lightning began to come one on top of the other with a speed that was frightening. My sister went for the Frenchman to wake him. \"Get up, Vitelle, quick,\" she cried, in French. \"Run up the bank for Mâtâ and Mr. Arthur — tell them to come and get us instantly.\"", "He did not answer her. He went down on his knees instead and began calling on the Virgin at the top of his voice.", "\"Don't wait for the Virgin, but go as quickly as possible. Do you not see we shall all be killed?\"", "\"Oh! not for the world, madame, not for the world,\" said Vitelle, pushing his head into a pack of furs, \"would I go up that bank in this storm.\" And from inside the furs he set about crying to every saint in the calendar.", "It was awful. The roar of the thunder and the flash of the lightning all round us were like a park of artillery firing without stopping. I got my cloak out, with some difficulty, and wrapped myself and Josette in it. Sister Margaret did the same for Edwin.", "\"Oh, madame,\" said the poor little girl, her teeth going with cold and fright, \"won't we be drowned?\"", "\"Very well,\" said my sister to the Frenchman. \"You see that Madame John is at the last agony — if you won't go for help I must, and Monsieur John must know that you left his wife to perish.\"", "That was more than Vitelle could carry. \"If I must, I must,\" he said, and with one desperate bound he was over the side and onto the shore and going up that hill for all he was worth.", "It was only a few minutes before a whole posse came flying down the bank, though it felt like an age from where we sat. The lightning was so constant that everything showed as plainly as in daylight. Mâtâ's curly hair was standing straight up off his head, and his eyes rolled at the sight of us with a kind of ghastly astonishment.", "\"Oh, my God, Madame Johns! what would Monsieur Johns say, to see you nows?\" he cried, and got me up in his arms and carried me up the hill. Arthur came behind with sister Margaret, and two of the others had Edwin and Josette. Nobody carried Vitelle. He had been careful not to risk his precious life by going down to the boat a second time.", "At the cabin where Arthur and Mâtâ had been lodged, a fire was got going, though not easily, and our trunks were brought up from the boat, and at last we were able to get out of our drenched clothes and the children out of theirs and into something better. Then we lay down on the clean, homely bed and slept until daylight."],
  p3s31d: ["Somebody had to learn how badly the furs had suffered, so an early start was proposed. The people in the cottages, however, had grown weary in well-doing. They would not make us a breakfast, and it made no difference that we promised to pay them well for the trouble. We went down to the water with very slender prospects of a morning meal.", "Then we saw the boat. Bedclothes, cloaks, trunks, the mess-basket, the packs of furs — every article of it carried the marks of a complete deluge, and the boat itself stood ankle-deep in water. There was not one place aboard where a person could sit or stand. We bailed for a while. We dragged out the packs of skins that had come off least badly and built them into a sort of divan amidships, and when that was done there was nothing left to do but climb in again and put up with what could not be helped.", "It was not an enviable position. Set down a hand or a foot anywhere and the water came up around it with a soft bubbling noise. As for the bandboxes and the work-baskets, I will not describe them. Breakfast was out of the question: the mess-basket, when opened, held one indistinguishable mass, tea and pepper and salt and short-cake all afloat together, and there was no separating any of it.", "And that was not the worst of it. The July sun climbed, fierce, and every object in the boat began to give off steam, until the air over us was nearly suffocating. The boat was old. The skins were old. Their company had been no pleasure on a dry day; now it was past bearing. There was nowhere on the river to go to get away from it, so we told the children to arm themselves with patience for the little of the voyage that was left.", "We sat on that odoriferous couch under a single umbrella, which was all the shade our whole party had against the scorching sun, and slid down the current through the long tedious day. One by one the landmarks came and went — the Kakalin, the Rapids, Dickenson's, the Agency — and we gazed at every human habitation we passed, thinking of the people inside with a roof over them and food in front of them, and counted over the hours that still lay between us and Fort Howard.", "The Frenchmen gave us no songs that day. Music and fasting keep poor company. In time we came in to Shanty-town, where the boat was to be unloaded, and every hand fell to carrying the cargo up to the Fur Company's warehouse by the landing. The work went quickly because they all worked with a will. And the moment it was finished, every voyageur among them made ready to walk away. Mâtâ stormed and raved to no purpose. Arthur remonstrated to no purpose.", "\"No,\" they said, \"they had brought the boat and cargo to the warehouse - that was all of their job.\" And they turned to go.", "\"Guardapié,\" I said, \"do you intend to leave us here?\"", "\"Bien, madame! it is the place we always stop at.\"", "\"Does Monsieur John pay you for bringing his family down?\"", "\"Oh, yes, Monsieur John has given us an order on the sutler, at the Fort down below.\"", "\"To be paid when you deliver us safe at the Fort down below. It seems I shall be there before you, and I shall arrange that matter. Monsieur John never dreamed that this would be your conduct.\"", "They put their heads together over that. What came of the conference was Guardapié and two others stepping back into the boat, taking up their oars, and rowing us the last two miles to Fort Howard, sulkily enough."],
  p3s32: ["It did not take us long to find out that Green Bay had worked itself into a panic over the Sauks. The town had settled on a conviction and would not be talked out of it: the enemy was coming here, on their way to Canada, to put themselves under the protection of the British Government. By what route they were to manage the last leg of it, nobody could say. Whether they meant to halt on the shore and build themselves bark canoes for the crossing, or whether they intended to charter one of Mr. Newbery's schooners, the good people had not quite settled in their minds. What was beyond dispute was that a share of the citizens had frightened themselves nearly to death, and had decided there was no safety anywhere on earth except behind the walls of the old fort — a fort falling apart at the seams, and emptied besides, since nearly every soldier in it had been pulled out and marched off to Fort Winnebago some time before.", "Then a traveler came through with a story, and the story did the rest. He had, he said, slept one night on the exact ground where the Sauks took their breakfast the next morning. Everyone knew the Sauks were down to very short commons by then. It followed, to the town's satisfaction, that half an hour's delay on his part would have seen him eaten, and the man was accordingly credited with a wonderful escape.", "Our own friends and acquaintances took no part in any of it. Anyone willing to think for a moment about the country such a march would have to cross, and about the numerous and well-armed whites waiting at the far end of it, could see the thing was impossible. Still, there were people no argument could reach, who believed safety lay in flight and nowhere else, and as the anxiety wound tighter the arrival of the \"Mariner\" was looked for more and more eagerly."],
  p3s33: ["The \"Mariner\" came in at last, early in the morning. Within an hour of her tying up, every house from one end of the settlement to the other had what she carried — \"the cholera was in this country! It was in Detroit - it was among the troops who were on their way to the seat of war! Whole companies had died of it in the river St. Clair, and the survivors had been put on shore at Port Gratiot, to save their lives as best they might!\" A savage enemy on one side of us and a pestilence on the other, and we were shut in between them.", "Anyone with people in the East took it hardest. It went through the place and left men hollowed out. The first to bring it to us was an officer whose courage no one had ever thought to question — he had proved it on the battle-field and proved it again in private ventures that asked more daring than most men have in them. He got the words out. Then he laid his head against the window-sill and wept like a child.", "Those who had to reach people near and dear to them went out again aboard the \"Mariner.\" The rest of us judged that where we already stood was the safer place, and we were right: the scourge never came to Green Bay that season.", "The heat that summer was punishing, and the mosquitoes came in such numbers that none of us so much as pretended to walk the parade after sundown without two fans, or green branches, kept swinging every moment to drive them off — which is, as it happens, the surest way in the world to bring them on. We had somehow let ourselves forget the flat indifference of Old Smoker, which used to astonish us: he would sit without moving and let his bare arms and body go literally gray with them, and then wipe off a handful, the blood following the track of his hand across the skin, and settle back and stoically wait for the chance to do it again. They say a mosquito left undisturbed until he has taken his fill leaves a far less inflamed bite than one brushed away in the middle of his feast.", "The daylight hours belonged to what is called the Green Bay fly, a species of dragon-fly. There are days when the outer walls of the houses carry so many that you cannot tell what color the wall beneath them is. Their lives are ephemeral things, scarcely more than a day long. Indoors you find the bodies stuck fast to the walls and the window-glass; outdoors they fall in such quantity that once a high wind has swept them into long rows against the sides of the quarters, a person can walk down them and toss them up underfoot like dry leaves in autumn."],
  p3s34: ["Crossing the parade, we would sometimes catch a sound that turned our heads — a tapping, over and over, on the bars of the dungeon where a criminal was being kept. The man doing it had murdered Lieutenant Foster.", "That amiable young officer may be remembered here: he had been our traveling companion the year before, on the journey up from Chicago. Some months after he reached Fort Howard he had occasion to send one of his own company, a soldier named Doyle, into confinement for intoxication. A few days later Doyle worked on the sergeant of the guard until the man agreed to walk him across to the quarters of Lieutenant Foster, the plea being that he wished to speak with him. Up he went, by the stairs, toward the young officer's room. The sergeant and another soldier waited at the bottom of them, near the door.", "Doyle stepped inside and addressed Lieutenant Foster. \"Will you please tell me, lieutenant, what I am confined for?\"", "\"No, sir,\" replied the officer, \"you know your offense well enough, return to your place of confinement.\"", "The man went down the stairs at a run, wrenched the gun out of the sergeant's hand, carried it back up, and fired it into Lieutenant Foster's heart.", "The lieutenant turned toward his inner apartment. \"Ah me!\" he said, and fell dead before he reached the entrance.", "A civil court had tried Doyle, and he lay now under sentence, waiting for the day of his execution. He was a hardened villain. Not once, in all that time, did he show the slightest compunction for his crime.", "Major Clark, the commanding officer, sent one day to ask what he might want for his comfort.", "\"If the Major pleased,\" he answered, \"he should like to have a light and a copy of Byron's Works.\"", "There was some fear that he would contrive to make away with himself before the appointed day, so everything that might have served him for a weapon was taken from him. His food was brought to him in a wooden bowl. A piece of broken crockery, they reasoned, would be quite enough for a man bent on destroying himself.", "Then, one morning, a little package came out from him to the commanding officer, offered as a present. In it was a rope — a strong one, twisted out of strips of his blanket that he had separated one by one with great care — and a large stout spike made fast at the end of it. The message that went with the gift was this: \"He wished Major Clark to see that if he chose to put an end to himself, he could find means to do it in spite of him.\"", "He held that hardened frame of mind to the very end. As he was led out for execution and taken beyond the gate, he noticed a quantity of lumber lying there, lately collected for putting up a new Company's warehouse.", "\"Ah, captain, what are you going to build here?\" he asked of Captain Scott, the officer attending him.", "\"Doyle,\" his captain told him, \"you have but a few moments to live - - you had better employ your thoughts about something else.\"", "\"It is for that very reason, captain,\" he said, \"that I am inquiring - as my time is short, I wish to gain all the information I can while it lasts.\""],
  p3s35: ["We were not left wondering long about the people we had left behind us. Inside of two weeks Old Smoker came again, and he came carrying letters. My husband wrote that General Dodge was with him at Port Winnebago, and that Generals Henry and Alexander were at the Fort as well. The men and the horses were used up — they had been scouring the country after Black Hawk until there was little left in either — and the moment they were recruited enough to move, the whole force would go out again after him, up toward the head-waters of the Rock River. The Winnebagoes had brought in word lately, and by everything that word contained, that was where he would be found.", "He told us to put away every uneasiness on his account, and he raised the hope that he would come for us before long, or send. After that our minds sat quieter than they had.", "Not many days later I was told, one morning, that a lady was at the front door asking for me. I went down, and the lady was my friend Madame Four-Legs. Her joy at seeing me had its own language: she pressed her two hands together above her forehead and then parted them in a slow waving motion, and laughed, and patted me up and down the arms. Then she reached into her bosom and brought out the letter she had carried to me, which was from my husband, and which said this — \"Generals Dodge and Henry left here a few days since, accompanied by Paquette, they met the Sauks near the Wisconsin, on the 21st. A battle ensued, in which upwards of fifty of the enemy were killed - our loss was one killed, and eight wounded. The citizens are well pleased that all this has been accomplished without any aid from Old White Beaver. [56] The war must be near its close, for the militia and regulars together will soon finish the remaining handful of fugitives.\""],
  p3s36: ["Lieutenant Hunter had taken leave of absence for the purpose of escorting us, and once he arrived our return to Fort Winnebago came together quickly. There was no Mackinac boat to be had. What we got instead was a Durham boat, which is longer than the other and shallower, and which offers nowhere to rig an awning or any other sort of shelter over the middle of it. Its one advantage was room, and we needed room: besides our own family the party took in Lieutenant and Mrs. Hunter, the wife of another officer then stationed at Fort Winnebago, and our cousin, Miss Forsyth. We understood, before we ever pushed off, that we were going to be packed in tight.", "The crew was made up half of Frenchmen and half of soldiers. Everything ready, we started on a fine bright morning toward the end of July.", "The second day was rowing and poling by turns, and early in the afternoon it brought us to the Grande Chûte.", "The custom there is to put the passengers ashore at the foot of the rapids. They climb the high bank and walk around the fall on dry ground while the crew hauls the boat up through the white water.", "Most of our people had already stepped out onto the shore when an idea took hold of one of the other ladies and me at the same moment.", "\"Let us stay in the boat,\" said we, \"and be pulled up the Chûte.\" The others went on up, and the two of us sat where we were and watched the men get ready, which was worth watching. They went over the side into the river, made a strong rope fast to the bow, and set themselves to it, every man pulling, walking slowly forward with all that weight behind them. Nothing had been taken out of the boat. The cargo was only our trunks and our stores, and it did not amount to much.", "Up we went. Every so often the hull found a rock that had been hidden from us and took a tremendous bump off it, and the spray came over us while the water roared and boiled along the sides.", "The men held in as near as they could to the high, sheer bank, where the current runs smoothest. At the head of the cordel was a merry simpleton of a Frenchman who kept turning his head to grin back at us, delighted that we were delighted.", "And we were in high glee. \"Isn't this charming?\" cried one. \"I only wish - - \"", "Whatever the wish was, a shout and a crash took the end off it. \"Have a care, Robineau! Mind where you are taking the boat!\" That was the cry, and it came too late. The man at the head of the line had been giving his attention to the ladies instead of to his work, and he had walked us straight into a sharp branch of a tree that leaned out from the bank. One pull tore the side out of the boat, and the water began to come in.", "My companion and I went off onto the nearest rocks that stood clear of the foam. Our screams and the men's shouting brought Lieutenant Hunter and some Indians down off the bank at a run, and they took us up in their arms and carried us in to land, while the crew set to work lustily fishing our things out of the water, every one of them soaked through.", "We climbed the high bank in a miserable state and joined the general lamentation over what the accident was likely to have cost us.", "\"Oh! my husband's new uniform!\" cried one, and \"Oh! the miniatures in the bottom of my trunk!\" sighed another - while, \"Oh! the silk dresses, and the ribbons, and the finery!\" formed the general chorus.", "Not one of us gave a thought to the provisions, though on our way in to shore we had all seen the barrel of bread and the tub of ice, which Lieutenant Hunter had providently laid in for our food and drink, riding away from us on the dancing water. Among the boxes carried ashore and \"toted\" up the steep bank was one holding loaves of sugar and packages of tea that I had bought from the sutler at the post to last us the winter. The young Indian who had brought it up set it down, and then called my attention to a thick white stream running out of the corners of it. I made signs for him to taste it. He put his finger in, and when he understood what it was he called out in delight to the others. Then I pointed at his hatchet and made a motion of opening the box. He did not need to be asked twice; the box was in pieces in no time.", "Then I beckoned up all the other youngsters, who had been standing about full of wonder, and what followed was such a scrambling and shouting that we were all in fits of laughter, the boys most of all. Bowls, dippers, bare hands, anything that would carry even a little — everything was pressed into service. The squaws were the quickest of all. Those with nothing else to hand tore off the stoutest pieces of the blue paper the sugar had been wrapped in and used that, and in a trice there was nothing left on the ground but the wet yellow bundles of tea and the splinters of the box that had held it.", "Fires had been lit by then, and one by one the contents of the trunks came out and went over every shrub and bush within reach. As luck had it, the box with the new uniform in it had been stacked high above the rest in the middle of the boat, and had taken very little harm. The rest of the wardrobes were a sorry business.", "There was not a white article left among them. Everything had gone mottled — blue, green, red and black running together in streaks, and dripping steadily from every end and corner.", "Then the rain started, rain having a fine instinct for the wrong moment, and the half-dried clothes had to be pulled out of the smoke and bundled away again in a condition to discourage anybody.", "The tent went up wet. The blankets were wrung out, dried as far as they could be dried, and laid on the ground for us to sleep on.", "A Hamburg cheese out of my stores was voted to me for a pillow. Supper's best feature was a piece of one of the wet loaves, saved because its barrel had been wedged in too tight to float away, and after that we took ourselves off to our repose."],
  p3s37: ["Morning came up hot and close. The rain had held the mosquitoes off all night; now they came back to collect what they were owed, and they tormented us without mercy.", "Breakfast was a miserable business and barely worth eating. That left the question of what on earth to do with ourselves. Our boat was still under water where she had gone down, below the hill and half-way up the rapids. As for the Waubanakee houses, the nearest of them stood miles away, and nothing would carry us there but an Indian canoe, and only supposing one of our present friends and neighbors chose to be obliging enough to fetch one for our use. Even granting that, whether boats enough existed to move a party as numerous as ours back to Green Bay was another matter entirely.", "We were still turning it over when a whoop came from the far side of the hill, where the ground falls away northward at the top of the rapids.", "\"There is John! that is certainly his voice!\" — the cry went up from several of the company at once.", "And it was my husband. A moment later he was standing in the middle of us. No arrival was ever better timed, or more plainly providential.", "He knew nothing of our plans; the unsettled state of the country had made it impossible to send him word. He had simply come with a boat, meaning to carry us home to Fort Winnebago.", "That finished the drying we had begun again that morning. Everything was bundled up as fast as hands could move, and in an incredibly short time we had been shifted into his boat, which was waiting above the Chûte out of the current, and the oars were taking us up toward Winnebago Lake.", "Our plan went no farther than the neighborhood of the lake, where a breeze would take the edge off the mosquitoes and we could make one more attempt on our clothing. So we put in at a fine high bank near the Little Butte and unpacked the trunks again, and before long there was something hanging from every bush and twig within reach — whatever the cruel waves had left us.", "The last rag and ribbon had barely gone out when we heard horses coming, and after them shouting and cheering that rang in through the forest.", "Twenty-five horsemen or so rode straight into the middle of us, banners up, veils streaming from their hats, arms flashing in the sun, and between the greetings and the roars of laughter they demanded to know the nature and the reasons of the singular confusion they had found us in.", "Colonel Stambough and Alexander Irwin had come out of Green Bay at the head of a company of young volunteers, with a whooping band of Menomonees behind them, the whole party making for the seat of war. We consoled them with the news that every victory had been won by now and every scalp taken. They said they hoped a few laurels might be left to earn yet, bade us adieu, and pressed on at speed.", "A clear summer moon lit Lake Winnebago for our crossing. The air was soft and just enough of it stirred to fill the sail, so the men were spared the oars.", "None of that witchery was enough to make us give up our rest after a day of heat and torment, and we arranged ourselves early to be stowed away in the middle of the boat. Not one of the numerous company could afterward explain how the thing was managed. Had any accident disturbed the arrangement, putting us back would have been a Chinese puzzle. The men lying along the outside had far the better of it, and we envied those off watch, who could snore and shift about whenever the humor took them.", "Powell's came up exactly in time for us to go ashore and cook breakfast, supposing we had owned anything to cook. We had counted on supplies there. Until then we had eaten our way through what was left of my husband's ample stock, and it had got so low that when the mess-basket could not be filled at that place, talk began of putting ourselves on allowance.", "Plenty of the wet bread remained, and it did what wet bread does under a July sun, and fermented. The tea went the same road; we had spread it on newspapers and bits of board and dried it carefully, and it turned musty and useless regardless. That left salted meat and a few crackers — literally nothing else, and hardly enough of that for the day we were standing in.", "So the men were pressed for every mile they had in them, the object being to make Gleason's, at Lake Puckaway, in good season the next day.", "In the evening we took our tea ashore in a lovely little clearing among the trees, and there our old enemies were worse than we had yet had them. We sat in cloaks and gloves with our veils tied close about the throat, and got a cracker or a swallow of tea in under the veil by stealth when we could.", "The men rowed well and had us at Gleason's by eleven the next day. La Grosse Américaine, my old friend, who had moved there from Bellefontaine, greeted us with the most enthusiastic demonstrations.", "\"Oh, Mrs. Armstrong,\" cried we, \"get us some breakfast - we are famishing!\"", "And then who should drive up but our faithful Mâtâ, in the old calèche that used to carry us on our little excursions about the Port. He had come over on the chance of meeting us, supposing some of the party would rather finish the journey home on wheels.", "He had thought ahead as well — tea, roasted coffee, fresh butter, eggs and the rest of it, in case such luxuries had run out on us so far along in the trip.", "That \"Good-morning, Madame Johns! How do you dos?\" of his fell on us like music.", "We could not wait for breakfast to be cooked. We crowded round her like so many children while she cut bread and spread the butter on it for us.", "Once the regular meal was over, we settled it that sister Margaret would take Josette and go back with Mâtâ to open the house and put it in order for the rest of us. It had served the whole time we were gone as head-quarters for militia, Indians and stragglers of every description, and it was easy enough to imagine that some degree of \"misrule and unreason\" had held sway there for the duration.", "Seventy-two miles still lay ahead of us, counting every bend the river takes — first across the beautiful water of Lac de Boeuf, then in among the low marshy country that runs out toward the Portage. One of the gentlemen decided to work up a small fright among the ladies as we came near the place where people had supposed the Sauks might cross on their way into the Chippewa country.", "\"Who knows,\" he said, gravely, \"but they may be lurking in this neighborhood yet? If so, we shall probably have some signal. We must be on the alert!\"", "One or two of the ladies lost their color and began looking about them. Then came a stretch of perfect silence, and out of it a low, drawn-out whistle. There was so much agitation, and in one or two cases such genuine terror, that the man behind the mischief had to own up on the spot and take a hearty scolding for the fright he had given.", "The second day out from Gleason's brought us to our own door just before sunset. The place shone with neatness and good order. Our good Manaigre and his wife had given efficient help: whitewash from the roof down to the door-sill, a scrubbing and cleansing right through, the carpets out of their wrappings and laid on the floors, the furniture back where it belonged, and — last, and by no means least — a noble supper standing on the board and steaming by the time we had got ourselves into a civilized toilet again.", "A good many friends from the Fort had come to meet us, and I doubt a happier or more thankful party has often been gathered under one roof."],
  p3s38: ["By then everyone was calling the war over. Word had reached us of the Bad Axe, where the regulars, the militia and the steamboat Warrior together had made a finish of the handful of Sauks still left, and with the news the hearts and the houses of the frontier settlers came back to rest.", "It may seem strange that an enemy so few in number, with so little to fight with, could throw a whole country into a panic and take such an enormous force to put down. The whole of the trouble was that nobody ever knew where to find them, either to strike at them or to guard against them. At the outset, I suspect, every military man felt exactly as that fine old veteran General Brady felt. \"Give me two infantry companies mounted,\" he said, \"and I will engage to whip the Sauks out of the country in one week!\"", "True enough. But before you can whip an enemy you have to meet him, and to follow Indians to any purpose and take them requires a particular kind of schooling — one that in those days hardly a man could claim, the frontier militia included.", "Through parts of that campaign there was a second trouble: the two branches of the service would not act as one. The regulars looked down on the militia's unprofessional way of going at things; the militia jeered at the slow, empty formalities of the regulars. Each side held it as an article of faith that the work would go better with the other out of it, and the militia, being quick to move, now and then took the business into their own hands and produced defeat and disgrace, as they did in the affair called \"Stillman's Run.\"", "The contempt the army officers felt for the militia ran downhill to their servants and dependants. The Ranger officers had paid their visit to Fort Winnebago before the battle of the Wisconsin, and afterward the officer of the mess that had entertained them called up his servant one day to go through the sutler's accounts. It was the same small \"Yellow David\" who had formerly been Captain Harney's.", "\"David,\" said the young gentleman, \"I see three bottles of cologne-water charged in the month's account of the mess at the sutler's. What does that mean?\"", "\"If you please, lieutenant,\" said David, respectfully, \"it was to sweeten up the dining-room and quarters after them milish' officers were here visiting.\""],
  p3s39: ["Black Hawk got away northward with a handful of his warriors, and not long afterward the One-eyed Day-kau-ray and his party ran him down and delivered him as a prisoner to General Street at Prairie du Chien. The women and children of his band had already been dealt with another way — loaded into canoes and pushed down the Mississippi, on the hope that somebody would let them cross and rejoin their tribe.", "Somebody had lashed the canoes together. A great many of them went over, and the children went into the river, and their mothers were too weak and worn out to reach them. Those who lived were taken prisoner and brought up to Prairie du Chien, starving and wretched. Our mother was at the Port when they came in. She had never seen people brought so low. One woman had a little Chippewa, and with it she laid out for our mother what the band had suffered and endured. It was appalling.", "They had eaten the horses they could spare. After the horses it was acorns, and elm-bark, and at the end of it grass. Many died of starvation, and the whites coming up behind them found the bodies lying along the trail. The woman who spoke to our mother had lost her husband in the fighting and every one of her children in the canoe that went over, and the only thing she wanted now was to go where they had gone. Poor Indians! who can wonder that they don't love the whites?"],
  p3s40: ["We had hardly settled back into the quiet of our own house before a summons overtook my husband. He was to gather the principal chiefs of the Winnebagoes and bring them down to Rock Island, to meet General Scott and Governor Reynolds. A treaty was to be laid before them there, for the purchase of every acre the tribe held east and south of the Wisconsin. Runners went out to the principal men. Whoever chose to present himself came, and with that company at his back my husband started.", "Two weeks passed. I had begun counting off the days that still had to go by before it was reasonable to look for him, and one afternoon I walked over to the Fort to sit with my sister. I had just stepped into the long hall of one range of quarters when Lieutenant Lacy came through from the far end, moving fast, and barely checked his stride enough to speak. \"Bad news, madam! Have you heard it?\"", "\"No. What is it?\"", "\"The cholera has broken out at Rock Island, and they are dying by five hundred a day. Dr. Finley has just arrived with the news.\" Then he was gone, before I could get a single question out of him.", "Cholera at Rock Island. My husband at Rock Island. I ran the length of the hall to the far door, the one that opens on the parade-ground. A sentinel was pacing not far off. \"Soldier,\" I called to him, \"will you run to the young officers' quarters and ask Dr. Finley to come here for a moment?\"", "He shook his head at me. He was not permitted to leave his post.", "A minute later Mrs. Lacy's servant-girl came out of a door under the steps. She was a worthless creature; but help was so hard to come by in that country that no lady could afford to keep a scrupulous tariff of moral qualification.", "\"Oh! Catharine,\" I said, \"will you run over and ask Dr. Finley to come here a moment? I must hear what news he has brought from Rock Island.\" She composed her face into something modest and answered, \"I don't like to go to the young officers' quarters.\"", "The hypocrisy of it made me furious, and the waiting was worse than the fury, and then, to my great joy, Dr. Finley appeared.", "\"Where is my husband?\" cried I.", "\"On his way home, madam, safe and sound. He will probably be here tomorrow.\" Then he told me what the disease was doing among the troops. It was bad — severe enough by any measure — but not the wholesale dying that the first report had made of it.", "The doctor was right that he was coming and wrong that he was out of danger. My husband was seized with cholera on the road. Paquette nursed him, and the poor woman at whose cabin he had stopped kept the chicken-broth going into him, and between the two of them he was soon over it. He reached home whole. He had gone round by Prairie du Chien on his way, and he brought his mother back with him again to her own home.", "The Indians had consented to sell their beautiful country. In such a case there is never really a choice to make. If they hold on to the land, the white settlements close in around them and hem them in, and they are worse off than if they had given it all up at the outset. They know this as well as anyone. So as a rule they let the land go when the Government asks for it, and spend what strength they have on getting the best bargain they can out of the asking. Here the equivalent was a tract of land[57] running back into the interior of Iowa, and ten thousand dollars a year besides.", "One article of the treaty asked something further of them. The Winnebagoes were to give up certain of their own people, men accused of having taken part with the Sauks in some of the murders along the frontier, so that our laws could try them, and acquit them or punish them as the case might be."],
  p3s41: ["Wau-kaun-kah — the Little Snake — offered himself. No one asked it of him. He would stand as hostage until the suspected men were handed over, and on those terms the Agent accepted him, and he was walked across to the Fort and shut up there to wait until the seven accused came to buy him back.", "Getting those seven to give themselves up was not quick work, and the nation had to do it themselves. What was being asked of them was that they walk into the hands of the whites and take whatever the white law decided to call justice. They remembered Red Bird. They remembered what his trial had been, and how he had wasted away and died in confinement. A man needs a good deal of nerve to step into that, and he needs to be very certain in himself that he has done nothing.", "So it was done with talk, and with more talk. The nation would not compel anyone. They had given their word, but they would not use force on their own people to make it good.", "While that went on, a formal council was held with the head chiefs at the Agency. Most of the nation was camped close by, having come in on word from Governor Porter, who was Governor of Michigan Territory and by that office also Superintendent of the Northwest Division of the Indians. Custom had always been for the Agent to receive the annuity money and hand it out. This year the Governor had let it be known that he would carry the money to Fort Winnebago himself and stand there while it was paid. The date he had named had now come round, and the main body of the Winnebagoes had come with it.", "Those who had not been at Rock Island, who had had no hand in signing their country away, said what they thought of it loudly, and grieved over it loudly. Loudest of all was Wild-Cat, the Falstaff of Garlic Island and the country round about. And who could blame him for the tears he shed. He had lost his home on the blue water of Winnebago Lake, and it had been a beautiful one.", "\"If he hadn't been accidentally stopped,\" he said, \"on his way to the treaty, and detained until it was too late, he would never, never have permitted the bargain.\"", "His Father knew exactly what had stopped him, which was a spectacular carouse somebody had lured him into along the road, and he answered with a perfectly straight face, \"That he had heard of the chief's misfortune on this occasion. How that, in ascending the Fox River, a couple of kegs of whiskey had come floating down the stream, which, running foul of his canoe with great force, had injured it to such a degree that he had been obliged to stop several days at the Mee-kan, to repair damages.\"", "The council came apart laughing, and it was so contagious a noise that Wild-Cat had no choice but to laugh along with the rest and let his misfortune stand as a joke.", "The accused men had by then retained Judge Doty to defend them when they came to trial, and at last word arrived: on a named day they would be brought to the Portage and given up to their Father, who would pass them on to the military officer appointed to take them.", "For poor Wau-kaun-kah that was the best news in the world, because it meant the door would open. We never once crossed inside the walls of the Fort without being hailed from the guard-room window, where he kept his post:", "\"Do you hear anything of those Indians? When are they coming, that I may be let out?\"", "We did what could be done. We saw that he had plenty to eat, and his Father and Paquette came to sit with him now and then. The Fort itself treated him well enough. None of it made any difference. Being shut in was a misery past any describing."],
  p3s42: ["It was a bright morning in autumn when word reached the authorities: the chiefs of the nation meant to come in to the Agency and hand over the suspected men as prisoners to the Americans.", "By ten o'clock there was something to see on the Portage road. A crowd was moving along it toward us. At that distance we could make out strong color, and the glitter of arms; as the crowd came nearer we could make out something else as well, white objects of a kind we did not know.", "On the platform before the door of our mansion, Mr. Kinzie was joined by General Dodge, by Major Plympton, and by an officer or two besides. They took their chairs there and waited to receive the party. The rest of us took the window, where there was both seeing and hearing to be had.", "The line came up the hill and turned toward us at a slow march. It was a grand and solemn sight. In front walked a number of the principal chiefs in their most brilliant array. Behind them came the prisoners. Every one of them was clothed in white cotton, in token of their innocence, and belted at the waist with a girdle. A drum and the shee-shee-qua kept time beneath the death-song they were chaunting. They had put on no paint and no ornament of any kind. Their faces were grave and thoughtful, and the moment had earned that gravity: what they knew of the customs of the whites amounted to very little, and that little was not of a sort to make a man cheerful. Nothing but their Father's word that they should have strict justice would have been likely to bring them to meet the engagements of the nation in this fashion.", "Behind them again came the remainder of the procession, a long train of Winnebagoes turned out in their holiday garb.", "The chiefs came up first, and the gentlemen who were standing ready gave them their hands. The prisoners came after, and the officers greeted them in the same way. Then the prisoners held out their hands to their Father, and he would not take them.", "\"No,\" he said. \"You have come here accused of great crimes - of having assisted in taking the lives of some of the defenceless settlers. When you have been tried by the laws of the land, and been proved innocent, then your Father will give you his hand.\"", "The words made them graver still. They seemed to find in them a sign that their Father also held them guilty. They drew back a little and sat down on the ground in a row, saying nothing, facing him and the officers. The other Indians settled into a circle about them, all but the one-eyed chief Kau-ray-kau-say-kah, the White Crow, on whom the duty of handing the prisoners over to the Agent had been laid.", "He spoke. His countrymen, he said, \"asserted their innocence of the charges preferred against them, but were quite willing to be tried by the laws of white men. He hoped they wouldn't be detained long, but that the matter would be investigated soon, and that they would come out of it clear and white.\"", "The answer given him was that the whole business would be conducted fairly and impartially, precisely as it would be conducted if the accused were white men; and to that was added a hope: that they would prove to have been good citizens and true, peaceful children of their Great Father, the President.", "With that concluded, White Crow asked leave for one thing more. He had a medal from the President, given him as a mark of friendship, and he wished to transfer it to his son, who stood at his side. The nation had already chosen the young man to fill the office of chief, which the father wanted resigning. Paquette interpreted the speeches made on the occasion. The son's demeanor was modest, the father's manner dignified and, throughout, full of feeling; together they made the ceremony a highly impressive one. Then the old chief lifted the medal from his own neck and hung it about his son's, and spoke him a few words suited to the moment. I think there was no one present who saw it unmoved.", "All through these ceremonies I had been reading the countenances of the prisoners where they sat on the ground in front of me. With one exception they were open and calm and had in them the look of conscious innocence. Of that one exception I could not deny that reasonable doubts might be entertained. One of them was remarkably fine-looking. Another was a boy of seventeen at the very most, and while the medal was passing from the one neck to the other he turned his eyes from father to son, and listened to what the speakers uttered, with an air and an expression of interest and satisfaction that were almost a child's.", "The ceremonies over, a file of soldiers conducted them away and committed them to the dungeon of the guard-house, to be held there until such time as they should be summoned before the court appointed to try their cause. Our hearts felt sad for them."],
  p3s43: ["The ceremonies of the surrender were gone through, and still the Indians did not disperse. They stayed on near the Portage, camped in the constant expectation of the annuity money they had been summoned there to receive. The day on which Governor Porter was to set out and bring it was put off a week, and then another week, and then another. Had he been able to foresee the whole train of evils that delay was going to set going, he would possibly have kept his appointment more promptly.", "There were several reasons why an early payment mattered. The Winnebagoes had been driven off their own grounds that spring by their anxiety to avoid any appearance of fraternizing with the Sauks, and so they had planted nothing that year. No gardens. No corn-fields. They had no provisions in hand either for the day they were living in or for the winter coming, nothing but their scanty stores of wild rice, and the rice was going down while they sat at the Portage and waited. If the hunting failed through the winter and the spring, they would have nothing at all.", "In the second place, the rations the Agent had been permitted by the Government to deal out to them now and then had been cut off. The Commissary had run short. The frequent levies of militia through the summer campaign, and the garrison's reinforcement by troops out of Fort Howard, had drawn so heavily on the stores at this post that everything issued now had to be counted out with the most rigid economy.", "My husband had seen it coming. As soon as the war ended he commissioned Mr. Kercheval, then sutler at Fort Howard, to get him two boat-loads of corn for distribution among the Indians. Unfortunately no corn at all could be had in Michigan. It had to be brought from Ohio, and in those days business was never done in a hurry; and by the time it finally reached Green Bay the navigation of the Fox River had closed over it. It sat there. It would be brought up the following spring.", "Every day wore on and the silver did not appear. At last their Father advised them to scatter to their hunting-grounds and get food, with the promise that they should be summoned the instant Governor Porter arrived. They took the advice and went.", "While they had been in our neighborhood they had asked, more than once, for leave to dance the scalp-dance in front of our door. I know nothing that can be imagined to match it. The scalps are stretched over small hoops or frames and carried at the ends of slender poles, and these are brandished about through the figures of the dance, with cries and shouts and furious gestures. The women begin as spectators. Then the scene takes them, and the music, which their own discordant notes are making more deafening by the minute, and they rush in, seize the scalps out of the hands of the owners, and toss them frantically about, screaming and yelling. I have seen as many as forty or fifty scalps in a single dance. On one occasion a man came quite near me carrying his, and I looked up, and saw that the hair on it was long and fair, and had plainly belonged to a woman. Another carried the skin of a human hand, stretched and dressed with all the care anyone would give a costly jewel. Sometimes they danced by moonlight. Those were the worst."],
  p3s44: ["Among so many painful events there were now and then some that came very near the ludicrous.", "One evening, sitting at tea, we heard guns firing off in the direction of the Wisconsin. Everyone was on their feet at once, making instinctively for the garrison. We came out of the house into a bluff and a meadow already in full commotion — Indians running toward the sound with their guns and spears laid across their shoulders, squaws and children standing out in front of their lodges and staring anxiously toward the noise that nobody could account for, and knots of French and half-breeds doing exactly what we were doing, running for the bridge to get themselves inside the pickets that had been put up so lately.", "One company of Indians came past us at speed, and a weapon carried carelessly caught one of our party on the side of the head. \"Oh!\" she shrieked, \"I am killed! an Indian has tomahawked me!\" She was reassured only by discovering that she could still run as fast as the best of us.", "When we were inside the Fort and standing on the parade-ground we could not help laughing at the figures we cut. Some of us had come without hats or shawls. Others had bundles of valuables snatched up in the moment of leaving. One was still holding her piece of bread-and-butter, not having had the presence of mind to put it down before she took to flight.", "The alarm turned out to have come from a party of Winnebagoes out of one of the Barribault villages. They were about to leave their home for a time, and were going through the ceremony of burying the scalps that they and their fathers had taken.", "Like the military funerals of civilized nations, their solemnities closed with the discharge of several volleys over the grave of their trophies."],
  p3s45: ["At last, about the beginning of November, two months after the time appointed, Governor Porter came in with the annuity money, and Major Forsyth and Mr. Kercheval with him. The Indians were assembled again, the payment was made, and they laid in a larger quantity of ammunition than usual — they understood well enough that only a good hunt would make up what was already lost — and then they set off for their wintering grounds.", "We were changing our own quarters about then, to our no small satisfaction. Through all the disturbances of the summer the new Agency House had been going quietly up — permission to build it had come from the Government at last, after a great deal of delay — and soon after the Governor and his party left, we moved into it.", "We had been settled a few weeks when Lieutenant Davies appeared one morning, just as we were sitting down to breakfast, with consternation all over his face. The Indian prisoners had escaped from the black-hole. The commanding officer, Colonel Cutler, wanted Mr. Kinzie to come over to the Fort and counsel with him about what was to be done.", "They had probably begun work very soon after they were put down there — the black-hole is a dungeon in the basement of the guard-house. What they noticed first was the regularity of it: meals brought three times a day, and in between, no one at all. So they went at the wall with their knives, and the earth they drew out they scattered thin across the floor of the prison. A blanket went over the opening, and one of the company always sat on the blanket, in place well before the hour when the soldier who had charge of them was due. Every time he came the Indians were sitting quietly, smoking, in perfect order. There was never anything about them to excite suspicion.", "They had never read the memoirs of Baron Trenck. What they had done was watch the proceedings of the badgers. Profiting by that example, they shaped the opening as they went, working it in a spiral, and in about six weeks the end of it came up into the open air beyond the walls of the Fort.", "They left their blankets behind so as to be as little encumbered as possible in flight. It was bitter December weather. They went out into the woods and prairies in nothing but their calico shirts and leggings, and I can readily believe that hope and exultation kept them comfortably warm until they reached shelter among their friends.", "It would compromise our own reputation as loyal and patriotic citizens to say how much this news privately delighted us.", "The question was how to get the fugitives back. The Agent could promise no more than to communicate with the chiefs and lay before them the wish of the officers — that the young men should give themselves up a second time, and so clear the men who had had charge of them of the imputation of carelessness the Government would otherwise be very likely to fasten upon them.", "On New-Year's Day many of the chiefs came in to the Agency, as their custom was, and their Father put the matter to them.", "They answered that if they saw the young men they would tell them what the officers would like them to do. Beyond that they could do nothing. They had kept their engagement; they had brought them in once and put them into the officers' hands. The Government had had them in its power and could not keep them. It must now go and catch them itself.", "The Government, having acquired a certain amount of experience the past summer in the business of catching Indians, wisely concluded to let the matter drop."],
  p3s46: ["Not long after, something happened that put our little community into a state of no small excitement. On a bitterly cold day Robineau came in to the Agency — the striker from the blacksmith establishment at Sugar Creek, over near the Four Lakes. He had come for a doctor. Mâtâ's eldest girl, Sophy, had been sliding on the lake, had gone down hard on the ice, and was badly hurt. Her father was not there to send for anyone himself; he had gone to Prairie du Chien to put his youngest daughter in school. Two or three days had already passed since the fall. A high fever had come up in her, and she was suffering a great deal, and so they had decided to send Robineau to us for advice and aid, and left Turcotte and a friendly Indian woman from a lodge nearby to sit with the girl.", "The matter was laid before the commanding officer. He did not think it prudent to let the surgeon go out of the post, but he gave leave of absence very cheerfully to Currie, the hospital steward, a young man who knew something of medicine and surgery both.", "Sophy would need experienced nursing, so we engaged Madame Bellaire, whose husband was the Frenchman generally employed as express to Chicago; and to help her and keep her company we added Agathe, a daughter of Day-kau-ray, who lived with Paquette's family.", "Of Agathe I shall have more to say in the future.", "The cold was severe when the four of them started out for Sugar Creek, some forty miles off. We had put up a good store of rice, crackers, tea and sugar for the sick girl, and this, along with what the party would need on the road, went packed on the horse Robineau had ridden in to the Portage. We looked for them to reach the place on the second day.", "Imagine our faces, then, when on the fourth day after they left it was Turcotte who came walking up, wanting to know why Robineau had not yet come back with help for Sophy. There was only one way to read it. Robineau had steered them exactly as well as he had steered the boat at the Grande Chûte the summer before; he had no water to wreck them in this time, so he had simply lost them in the woods or out on the prairie. The one consolation was that starving seemed unlikely — the rice and crackers were several days' worth — and Agathe would be used to this kind of living, and sooner or later they must come across Indians who would bring them back to us.", "The days went by and nothing came. Turcotte went back to Sugar Creek carrying comforts and prescriptions for Sophy, and Colonel Cutler sent out a party to search. Of the lost ones it was poor Currie we pitied most, his constitution being a delicate one.", "The snow came on and the winds howled, and there was nothing any of us could do but go from one window to the next and back again, watching the distance in hopes that somebody would appear in it. There were no Indians at hand to send after them. By the tenth day we had very nearly given up.", "That was when the word came, all at once: \"They are found! They are at the Fort!\" A party of soldiers out exploring had come upon them at Hastings's Woods, twelve miles from us, working their way home slowly and feebly. They had known they were on the right road at last. They had barely had the strength left to walk it.", "Their food had run out two days before. Cold and hunger had brought them to the point of discussing, in earnest, whether to kill the horse and eat him. What stopped them was Currie: he could not go on foot, and none of them could face leaving him in the woods to die.", "Agathe had known from the very beginning that the course they were on was wrong. Robineau, obstinate as ever, held to it anyway, and held to it until it brought them out on the Rock River, and then at last he admitted his mistake, and they turned and began working their way back.", "Agathe had her hatchet with her, as the custom of her people is. Because of that they had a fire every night and boughs cut to break the storms off them. Without it they must certainly have died out there.", "Two things about that journey stirred something in us stronger than sympathy. The first was what the miserable Robineau had done to Currie — demanded of him, first, all his money, and afterward his watch, as the price of leading the party back into the right path, which he swore he knew perfectly well.", "The second was what Bellaire did to his kind, excellent wife when she got home. He gave her a hearty flogging \"for going off,\" as he said, \"on such a fool's errand.\"", "Bellaire lay outside our jurisdiction. Mons. Robineau did not. He was discharged on the spot and told he might think himself a fortunate man to have escaped a legal process for swindling.", "I am glad to be able to say that Sophy Mâtâ, for whose sake all this suffering was gone through, was quite well again by the time her father came back from the Prairie."],
  p3s47: ["Agathe's father went by the name Rascal Day-kau-ray. Whether the name was deserved is a thing the future will have to decide. He was a brother of the grand old chief who carried that name, and about as different from him as two men out of one blood can contrive to be.", "The Day-kau-rays were a strikingly handsome family, and this daughter of theirs was the finest of them to look at — tall, strongly and well made, with a round sweet face and the low, musical voice the women of her people have. A stranger would have turned to look at her. What held you, though, was the sadness that sat in her face, which made you want, against your will, to know how it had come there. Mrs. Paquette was her friend, and it was from Mrs. Paquette that I learned it, and what she told me was a touching thing indeed.", "One of the young officers at the Fort had noticed her, and had fixed on her — I will not say his heart, since there is real doubt he owned one — but his mind. He went to Paquette and asked him to arrange what he was pleased to call a marriage. It shames me to write that Paquette agreed to it. He understood precisely what it meant to carry lies to Agathe's family. He understood precisely what he was about to do to her.", "She was already promised. There was a young man of her own people, and, as almost always happens, the feeling ran deep on both sides. These are people whose thoughts do not range far past the business of the day, and so what they love and what they hate become the very threads their character is woven from. Their feelings are fierce because they have so few things to spend them on. Family is the whole of it, and for the women it is everything they have.", "Marriage among them is sacred and cannot be undone. I have read, in a report made not long ago to the Historical Society of Wisconsin, that in earlier days a temporary marriage between a white man and a Menomonee woman was common enough and carried no disgrace with it. My fear is that if anyone went back and examined those cases closely, he would find deceit and misrepresentation laid on top of every other sin in the business, and would find the woman to have been a victim of the arrangement and not a willing party to it.", "Whatever was true elsewhere, nothing of the kind exists among the Winnebagoes. The strictness with which their women hold to propriety is one of the marked things about them. A woman who breaks it is spoken of as having \"forgotten herself,\" and her friends will cast her off and \"forget\" her in turn.", "So when the marriage was proposed between the young officer and Day-kau-ray's daughter, it was understood by everyone concerned to be a true one and a permanent one. The father would never have laid himself open to the scorn of his entire nation by selling his child to be some man's mistress. The Day-kau-rays, as I have mentioned before, took no small pride in the thread of French blood that ran, far back, into the native stream in their veins; and I imagine that when Agathe's father consented, the honor he believed he was gaining weighed with him quite as much as the valuable presents that came with the offer.", "However that may be, the girl was taken away from the man she loved, out of her father's lodge, and installed in the young officer's quarters.", "There were no ladies at the garrison in those days. Had there been even one, I doubt the thing would have been attempted. Set a man down far out in the wilderness, with nothing around him of religion or of decent society to hold him to his shape, and it is no great wonder if his sense of right goes dark in him, and he makes the old choice: \"Evil, be thou my good!\"", "Then came the second act, worse than the first for sheer cruelty. The officer left the post on furlough — that was his word for it — and he did not come back. In time the news arrived that he had married, and when he rejoined his regiment he did it somewhere else.", "The \"woe pronounced against him\" came from more than one tongue, and there was nothing strange in the strength of it. \"He will never,\" my informant said, \"dare show himself in this country again! Not an Indian who knows the Day-kau-rays but would take his life if he should meet him!\""],
  p3s48: ["Everything that had held poor Agathe to the world had been cut away, except the baby. She would not go back to her father's lodge. She had been abandoned, and to her mind that made her a dishonored woman. What was left of her was poured into one purpose: the child would be raised as a white child. She dressed it as the French children were dressed — a frock of bright calico and a cap made of the same stuff, edged with a narrow black lace. It was a beautiful baby. In all the time I knew her, the only smile I ever saw come to Agathe's face came when somebody in our family took the child up and praised it.", "That was taken from her too. We were away at Green Bay, and the Sauks were in the country then, and the child fell violently ill. Her home was the house at Paquette's, and the place was full of Indians coming and going, noise at all hours, no rest to be had in it. My husband had a room made ready for her in our own house, where it was quiet and where the post physician could attend her. None of it made any difference. The little creature could not be saved. Those who were there told me afterward what the mother was like, bent over the one thing on earth that belonged to her, and they said it was past bearing to watch. When they had to take the child out of her arms, it seemed more than a human being could survive. There were friends around her then, none of them her nation or her color, doing what they could. And the father — did he ever once spare a thought, or send so much as a question, about what became of his child, or about the girl whose whole life he had turned dark and empty? Let us hope that he did. Let us hope he repented, and asked pardon from above for the evil he had done.", "M. Mazzuchelli had baptized her. It may be that something of what she learned then stayed with her, and gave her some comfort in her grief, and stood in some small part for everything that had been blighted in her so early.", "Months after the child died she came to see me, with several of the half-breed women of the neighborhood, to pay her respects and to offer her congratulations on the arrival of the young Shaw-nee-aw-kee. She looked down at her \"little brother,\" as they all called him, and she took his soft, tiny hand into her own, and her eyes filled, and she said a few small tender words to him that told me plainly how full her heart was. I very nearly wept along with her — thinking of all that ruin and grief, and that it was the work of one man's selfishness."],
  p3s49: ["In the first days of February, 1833, my husband and Lieutenant Hunter, with one or two others along, started out for Chicago. The place had grown enough to call itself a town — there may have been fifty people in it — and that meant the owners of \"Kinzie's Addition\" could no longer put off laying out their lots and cutting streets through the property. The whole of that work was finished on this visit.", "They were out on the ground with a surveyor when my husband noticed a boy: a quick, bright-looking child in Indian dress, hopping along at the elbow of the assistant who carried the chain and calling out after him, in perfect imitation, \"Stick!\" \"stuck!\" every time the man did. He asked who the boy belonged to, and the answer took him aback. This was the younger brother of Victoire, Genevieve, and Baptiste, the servants who had been with the family for years. His name was Tomah. He had never in his life worn white clothing — a blanket and leggings were what he had — and he had never lived anywhere but in a wigwam. My husband asked whether he would care to come back with him to Fort Winnebago and learn to be a white boy. Tomah liked the sound of it very much, and his mother agreed to it, and so he was stowed into the wagon along with the two gentlemen and all their traveling gear for the journey home.", "He had been fitted out by then with a jacket and trousers and whatever else his new station and character required. They were coming up on the Aux Plaines, near the house of Glode (Claude) Laframboise, and Tomah knew there would be people there who knew him. He asked to be let down to walk a little way. The next the gentlemen saw of him he was a Pottowattamie again, dressed head to foot in the old way — bitter as the weather was, he had put that uncomfortable clothing back on rather than let his old friends see what had been made of him."],
  p3s50: ["When he reached Fort Winnebago the first thing we did was fit him out with a whole wardrobe of his own. It went into a box in the room where he slept, and the box was his to keep. I have no words for what that did to him. Every minute he could spare he spent looking at what was in it. Now and then Tomah would simply be gone, and he was never anywhere else: sitting on the floor by his little trunk, folding and refolding the clothes, laying them out lengthwise, then crosswise, the happiest creature alive.", "The next thing was to make him useful. We began with small jobs, the sort of thing we supposed he had done before, and it did not take us long to notice a pattern: the moment there was anything resembling work in the house, Tomah went to bed. He would go without his supper to do it. One evening, right at dark, he was handed some fish to scale, and Tom, true to himself, retired at once.", "The cook came in to ask what she was supposed to do about it. There was nothing for it but to call my husband in to interpret. He sent for Tomah, and when the boy came into the parlor Mr. Kinzie said to him, in Pottowattamie, \"There are some fish, Tomah, in the kitchen, and we want you to scale them.\"", "\"Now?\" said Tom, in complete astonishment. \"It is very late.\"", "Miss Rolette, a young lady who was staying with us and who had the language, could not hold it in and laughed outright at the plainness of the thing, and we all laughed with her, which made Tom look a little indignant. But once it was explained to him that scaling fish at night, and putting salt and pepper on them, is simply a white custom, he made his peace with the duty and went and did it.", "His next office was laying the table. We had a best service of china that only came out for company, and a best set of teaspoons that lived in a bureau drawer in my own room upstairs. I kept that drawer locked and the key under a little clock on the mantel-piece. The first time I showed Tomah how things were to be arranged for visitors, I carried the silver down and laid it out on the table with my own hands.", "Not long after, we were expecting company to tea again, and I told Tomah that the best china was to be used. So imagine what I felt when I passed through the dining-room a little later and found on the table not only the new china but the company silver as well. I asked our mother, who could talk with him, to look into it.", "Tomah answered her perfectly coolly. \"He got the silver where it was kept.\"", "\"Did he find the drawer open?\"", "\"No - he opened it with a key.\"", "\"Was the key in the drawer?\"", "\"No - it was under that thing on the shelf.\"", "\"How did he know it was kept there?\"", "That was the one question Mr. Tomah would not answer. We never did learn whether he had watched me at some point and taken note. Nobody had ever laid eyes on him in that part of the house. All the same, there was hardly an article under our roof whose whereabouts Tomah did not know, and when anybody was hunting for something the answer in our house became, \"Ask Tomah - he will tell you.\" He always could.", "The young officers found him an endless entertainment. We were giving a tea one evening for all the families and the young officers from the Fort, and to make Tomah look as professional as we could we made him a white apron with long sleeves to wear while he helped Mary and Josette carry the tea round — for I must own that Tomah's clothes were never kept in the order out of the trunk that they were kept in while they were in it.", "Tom was delighted with the costume and just as delighted with the work. He did it faultlessly, too; he never had the least trouble copying anything he had watched somebody else do. After tea there was music. I was standing beside the piano, where one of the ladies had sat down to play, when Lieutenant Vancleve said quietly at my elbow, \"Look behind you a moment.\"", "I turned round. There was Tom, seated between two of the company, as stately as a judge, his white apron smoothed flat over his knees and his hands clasped in front of him, listening to the music and plainly on the best possible terms with himself and with everyone in the room. Julian and Edwin could barely hold their faces straight, but neither of them dared say a word or make a move for fear of shifting him before the company had got the full good of the sight. It was carried unanimously that Tomah should be allowed to stay and taste the pleasures of society for one evening. Then the music stopped, and with that restlessness of his he was bored on the instant, and took his leave of the company without ceremony."],
  p3s51: ["As spring drew on, the suffering among the Indians that we had long looked for began to show itself. How far it had gone we learned from the ones who came to the door — small parties, a few at a time, asking for food.", "Their Father gave out rations for as long as there were rations to give. But the stores in the Commissary Department had run down so far that Colonel Cutler could not in conscience authorize more than the barest relief, and that only where the case was already desperate.", "We had been careful with our own stores all winter, afraid of reaching the bottom of the flour and the meal before more could be brought up from \"below.\" We had even bought a quantity of sour flour that the commissary had condemned; with enough saleratus worked into it and a fair proportion of potatoes, it made a bread we were glad enough to eat. But party after party came and stood before us and told us plainly what they were enduring, and we gave, and gave again, until the day came when there was nothing left in the house to hand over.", "The half-breed families near us had done as we had, sharing while their own stock lasted, and now they too were obliged to refuse. Their women came often to sit with us and repeat what was being carried in from the wintering grounds. The winter had been an open one. The snow had never lain deep enough at any time for the Indians to track deer, and in any case the game was gone; the troops and the war-parties had swept the country clean the summer before.", "They were dying in whole companies, we heard, of nothing but want of food, and lying stretched where they fell along the road to the Portage, which they had been trying to reach, dragging frames that had no strength left in them. For weeks together some had lived on soup boiled from the bark of the slippery elm, or on stewed acorns.", "Our own food came now as a daily ration from the garrison; matters had reached the point where a barrel of flour could not be had at one time. When a meal was finished I went into the pantry and gathered every last particle that remained, and set it aside, because someone would be at the door needing it, and someone always was."],
  p3s52: ["One day, while I was at that work, a face came to the window that I had known well once. It was the elder Day-kau-ray's pretty daughter. She used to come to us constantly and watch whatever we were doing with great interest — our sewing, our weeding and cultivating in the garden, our reading. That last I had tried many times to give her some notion of, opening the Family Bible to the plates and explaining them as well as I could. But I had lost sight of her for a long while. And how changed she was now. How wan. I said to her what I always said - \" Tshah-ko-zhah?\" (What is it?) - and what came back was a sigh with a sob most of the way inside it. She asked me for nothing. Her face said the rest.", "I picked up my dish and put it into her hands. I expected her to fall on it. She did not. She took it, made signs that she would come back soon, and walked away with it. When she brought it back to me I was nearly certain she had not swallowed a mouthful herself.", "Oh, those boats. The corn was on them. Why would they not come? We both wrote, and we sent word to hurry them, and it changed nothing — in those unenterprising times nothing and nobody could be hurried along. All we could be certain of was that the boats would come when they were ready, and not one moment sooner."],
  p3s53: ["Before long we were obliged to keep the doors and the windows fast, to shut out a sight of misery we had no means of relieving. If a door was opened to let in a member of the family, some wretched mother would push through it, take hold of my infant's hand and put the hand of her own famishing child inside it, and tell us, pleading, that he was imploring \"his little brother\" for food. The stoutest man alive could not have watched that with a dry eye, and it happened again and again. Screening the lower part of the windows with curtains did no good. They climbed up the outside, and tier upon tier of gaunt, wretched faces would rise above the curtain and look in at us, watching, to see whether we were truly as ill provided as we said we were.", "The noble old Day-kau-ray came in one day from the Barribault to tell us what state his village was in. More than forty of his people, he said, had now gone many days with nothing to eat but bark and roots. My husband took him to the commanding officer so he could tell his story himself and learn whether any quantity of food could be had from that quarter. The result was a promise of a small allowance of flour, enough to take the edge off the hunger of his own family.", "When this was explained to the chief, he turned away from it. No, he said. If his people could not be relieved, then he and his family would starve along with them. And he refused the offered succor, for those nearest and dearest to him, until every one of them could share alike."],
  p3s54: ["Then, at last, word came that \"the boats were in sight,\" and it was a thrilling thing to hear, and a joyful one.", "Hundreds of them stood along the bank, waiting for the boats. And how slowly they came — up the bends of that river, crawling across the width of the open prairie, while the people watched. Our own impatience was as bad as theirs, or nearly. Then the first boat touched the shore, and we could hardly keep from laughing out loud, because old Wild-Cat — a good deal less enormous than he used to be — caught up \"the Washington Woman\" in his arms and hugged her and danced her round in the pure ecstasy of the thing.", "Their Father gave them the sign, and every hatchet they had been holding ready so long came down at once. In almost no time at all the barrels were split open, one after another, and emptied of their corn. Even the smallest children filled pans and kettles with it and ran off to the fires that were burning all round us, to parch and cook what they had got.", "There was no more destitution after that day. The abundance in front of us was followed at once by the supplies coming in for the Commissary's Department, and our poor children, fed now and with the strength back in them, went off again to their own villages to get their crops ready for the season ahead."],
  p3s55: ["Somewhere in the middle of that spring the Rev. Mr. Kent and Mrs. Kent came up from Galena to see us. I mark the visit because of what it was: the first time the gospel was preached at Fort Winnebago according to the Protestant faith. They cleared the large parlor of the hospital and made it fit for a service, and we said it to one another with real gladness — \"Let us go to the house of the Lord!\"", "We had been living in that place close on three years with no public service of praise and thanksgiving to go to. This looked to us like the beginning of better times, and our little \"sewing-society\" bent to its work harder than before, building up a fund that might one day be enough to keep a missionary here for good.", "Not long afterward, on a fine spring morning, we were sitting at breakfast when a party of Indians came into the parlor and up to the door of the room where we were. Two of them walked on through and out onto the small portico. The third did not follow them. He stopped in the doorway where he had first shown himself, and stayed there. He was standing nearly opposite me. I lifted my eyes to him, and the clothes were not the clothes I had seen before, and the paint was laid over his face and body, and I knew him anyway, at once.", "I went on pouring the coffee, and while I poured I said to my husband, \"The one behind you, with whom you are speaking, is one of the escaped prisoners.\"", "Mr. Kinzie did not turn his head. He went on taking in every direction they had to give him about the guns and traps and the rest of it that they meant to leave with the blacksmith for mending. Then, while they were still talking, he turned toward the parlor door as though it meant nothing at all and answered the man who was speaking to him. When he spoke to me again, this is what he said. \"You are right, but it is no affair of ours. We are none of us to look so as to give him notice that we suspect anything. They are undoubtedly innocent, and have suffered enough already.\"", "Their Father did what he never did: he asked none of them their names. He wrote out their directions, tied each paper to the implement it belonged with, and told them to carry them to M. Morrin and deliver them themselves.", "The rest of our circle were delighted by the young fellow's audacity, and we very much wanted to tell the officers that if we had chosen to, we could have handed them one of their fugitives."],
  p3s56: ["It had come to that point at last: we began to talk in earnest about giving up the home we loved and settling ourselves at Detroit for a time, while the arrangements went forward for a permanent place at Chicago.", "When the news reached our Winnebago children, the grief of it was loud and it did not stop. They came in from all the country round about, wanting to know whether what they had heard was true, and begging us — pleading, in earnest — that we would stay and live and die among them.", "Of all of them, none was so broken by it as Elizabeth, our poor Cut-Nose. On the day we first told her, she sat down in one spot and stayed there hour after hour, taking the corner of the chintz shawl she wore pinned across her bosom to blot the tears as fast as they came down her face.", "\"No! I never, never, never shall I find such friends again,\" she would exclaim. \"You will go away, and I shall be left here all alone.\"", "Wild-Cat grieved too — fat, jolly Wild-Cat — and he made no attempt to grieve quietly.", "On the morning we left, he would not be satisfied until he had the baby in his arms and settled on his fat, dirty knee. \"Oh, my little brother,\" he told him, \"you will never come back to see your poor brother again!\"", "He had taken an extra glass for the occasion, and he cried like an infant.", "So on the morning of the 1st of July, 1888, with heavy hearts, we said our good-byes to the long cortége that had walked us down to the boat. She was waiting to carry us to Green Bay, where Governor Porter and Mr. Brush would meet us and take us on to Detroit under their escort.", "Their farewells finished, they turned about to go with their father across the Portage, on his road to Chicago. We watched them a long while, the line of them bending along the road, and we heard them wailing still — a parting they knew already was for good."],
};
